window.NOTES=[{"id":"intro","n":0,"title":"How to use these notes","marks":0,"html":"<p>Target 90 of 100: the exam is 100 one-mark questions in 2 hours, pass mark 50, no negative marking, so never leave a question blank. These notes follow the March 2026 revised workbook, which reflects the SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 2026 and the new expense limits from 1 April 2026.</p>\n<p><strong>What the real paper feels like.</strong> A candidate who sat the exam in October 2026 found it harder than recall drills suggest. About <strong>20 to 30 questions</strong> gave two or three statements and asked which is true or which is false. <strong>Fewer than 10</strong> needed any calculation. Several questions were \"tweaks\" that test whether you understand a concept, such as asking what the <strong>numerator</strong> of a ratio represents rather than its value. Learn each rule well enough to spot a near-miss: a swapped number, a swapped body (trustees vs AMC), or an added \"only\". Each chapter now ends with <strong>Exam traps</strong>, and Practice has a <strong>Statement drill</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Unit</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n<th>Exam weight</th>\n<th>Practice Qs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Investment Landscape</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>56</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>52</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Legal Structure of Mutual Funds</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>43</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Legal and Regulatory Framework</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Scheme Related Information</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>68</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Fund Distribution and Channel Management</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>41</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>NAV, TER and Pricing of Units</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>58</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Taxation</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Investor Services</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>101</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Risk, Return and Performance of Funds</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>53</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Mutual Fund Scheme Performance</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>53</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Mutual Fund Scheme Selection</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>100</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Chapters 9 and 12 carry 30 marks between them, and 4 and 5 another 20. Half the paper sits in four chapters.</p>\n<p><strong>Suggested two-week plan (one session a day):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Day</th>\n<th>Session</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Chapter 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Chapters 2 and 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Chapter 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Chapter 5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Chapter 6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Chapters 7 and 8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Chapter 9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Chapter 10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Chapter 11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Chapter 12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Mocks 1 and 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Mocks 3 and 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Mock 5 and weak-area revision</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Big changes in the March 2026 workbook.</strong> Know these cold:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 2026</strong> replace the 1996 Regulations. Schedule numbers moved: valuation = <strong>Seventh</strong>, ad code = <strong>Fifth</strong>, code of conduct = <strong>Fourth</strong>, investment restrictions = <strong>Sixth</strong>.</li>\n<li>New expense limits (<strong>base expense ratio</strong>): equity <strong>2.10%</strong> and non-equity <strong>1.85%</strong> on the first Rs 500 cr; index/ETF <strong>0.90%</strong>; brokerage <strong>0.06% / 0.02%</strong>; the extra <strong>0.05% is gone</strong>.</li>\n<li>Categories: equity <strong>13</strong>, debt <strong>17</strong>, hybrid <strong>7</strong>; <strong>Life Cycle Funds</strong> replace solution-oriented (retirement/children's) funds.</li>\n<li>Distributors earn <strong>trail commission only</strong>. Upfront commission and transaction charges were withdrawn by SEBI in August 2025.</li>\n<li>Investors may name <strong>up to 10 nominees</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"ch1","n":1,"title":"Investment Landscape","marks":8,"html":"<ul>\n<li>Objective + amount + timeline = <strong>financial goal</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hospitalisation, theft, fire: cover with <strong>emergency fund + insurance</strong>, not investments.</li>\n<li>Pool approach flaw: <strong>horizon unknown</strong>.</li>\n<li>Shalini: Rs 50 lakh at 8% for 10 years = <strong>Rs 1,07,94,625</strong>.</li>\n<li>Rs 30,000 at 6%: <strong>31,800</strong> (1 yr), <strong>53,725</strong> (10 yrs), <strong>96,214</strong> (20 yrs).</li>\n<li>Rs 10,000 at 8%: <strong>14,693</strong> (5), <strong>21,589</strong> (10), <strong>46,610</strong> (20), <strong>1,00,627</strong> (30); 100 units buys <strong>68</strong> after 5 yrs, <strong>46</strong> after 10.</li>\n<li><strong>Saving precedes investing</strong>; \"saving\" comes from \"safe\".</li>\n<li>Top 3 evaluation factors: <strong>safety, liquidity, returns</strong>.</li>\n<li>Exit penalty hits <strong>returns</strong>, not liquidity.</li>\n<li>Four asset classes: <strong>Real estate, Commodities, Equity, Fixed income</strong>.</li>\n<li>Real estate: <strong>location</strong> is key; illiquid; <strong>not divisible</strong>; self-occupied home = <strong>expense</strong>.</li>\n<li>Commodity derivatives aren't investments: <strong>leveraged + short-term</strong>.</li>\n<li>Only <strong>commodities</strong> give no intermittent cash flow; physical gold = no regular income.</li>\n<li>Bond bought at issue, held to maturity: <strong>no capital gain</strong>.</li>\n<li>Sensex: base <strong>100 in 1979</strong>, <strong>82,269 by 30 Jan 2026</strong>, <strong>\\~15.4% p.a.</strong> (before dividends).</li>\n<li>Only financial form: <strong>equity and bonds</strong>.</li>\n<li>Table 1.1: <strong>PPF, SSY, SCSS, POMIS, endowment/money-back = Fixed Income</strong>; <strong>REITs, InvITs, REMF = Real Estate</strong>; <strong>coins, art, stamps = Others</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Nominal</strong> = before inflation; <strong>real</strong> = after inflation.</li>\n<li>Listed equity is liquid but <strong>unfit for short-term needs</strong>.</li>\n<li>Credit risk causes: borrower's <strong>ability or intention</strong>.</li>\n<li>Own government's bonds = <strong>safest, lowest rate</strong>.</li>\n<li>Pager industry = <strong>industry-specific</strong> risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Market-wide risk cannot be diversified</strong>; company-specific can.</li>\n<li>Rates up, bond price <strong>down</strong>; <strong>longer maturity = bigger swing</strong>.</li>\n<li>Risk strategies: <strong>avoid, take a position, diversify</strong>; lay investor = <strong>diversify</strong>.</li>\n<li>Credit risk measured by <strong>rating + spreads</strong>; volatility by <strong>variance, SD, beta, modified duration</strong>.</li>\n<li>Dominant emotions: <strong>fear, greed, hope</strong>.</li>\n<li>Rs 5,000 loss hurts more than gain = <strong>loss aversion</strong> (Kahneman &amp; Tversky).</li>\n<li>Decide first, seek proof later = <strong>confirmation bias</strong>.</li>\n<li>\"Known devil vs unknown angel\" = <strong>familiarity bias</strong>.</li>\n<li>Bull run drives over-allocation = <strong>recency bias</strong>.</li>\n<li>IT employee with only tech stocks = <strong>interest of the investor</strong>.</li>\n<li>Remedy for biases: third opinion from an <strong>RIA or MFD</strong>.</li>\n<li>Risk profile = <strong>need + ability + willingness</strong>; ability = <strong>finances + horizon</strong>; willingness = <strong>psychology</strong>.</li>\n<li>Strategic = <strong>fixed target mix</strong>; tactical/dynamic = <strong>improve risk-adjusted return</strong>, for <strong>seasoned investors with large surplus</strong>.</li>\n<li>Rebalancing = <strong>sell what rose, buy what fell</strong>; 50:50 with A +20%, B −5% on Rs 1,000 each: <strong>sell Rs 125 of A</strong>.</li>\n<li>Passive breach rebalancing: <strong>30 business days</strong>; excludes index funds/ETFs; <strong>not applicable to overnight funds</strong>.</li>\n<li>DIY hidden costs: <strong>time + mistakes</strong>; 2% fee on Rs 10 lakh = <strong>\\~Rs 20,000/yr</strong>.</li>\n<li>A mutual fund is a <strong>different way of investing</strong>, not a different product.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li>An exit penalty hurts <strong>returns</strong>, not liquidity.</li>\n<li><strong>Market-wide</strong> risk cannot be diversified; only company-specific risk can.</li>\n<li>Ability to take risk = <strong>finances + horizon</strong>; willingness = <strong>psychology</strong>, not finances.</li>\n<li>Deciding first and seeking proof later = <strong>confirmation</strong> bias; 'known devil vs unknown angel' = <strong>familiarity</strong> bias; over-allocating after a bull run = <strong>recency</strong> bias.</li>\n<li>A self-occupied home is an <strong>expense</strong>, not an investment.</li>\n<li>REITs, InvITs and REMF = <strong>Real Estate</strong>, not equity; PPF, SSY, SCSS, POMIS and endowment policies = <strong>Fixed Income</strong>; coins, art, stamps = <strong>Others</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Commodities</strong> are the only asset class with no intermittent cash flow, not fixed income.</li>\n<li>Credit risk is measured by <strong>rating and spreads</strong>; variance, SD, beta and modified duration measure <strong>volatility</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"ch2","n":2,"title":"Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund","marks":6,"html":"<ul>\n<li>You invest <strong>through</strong> a mutual fund, not <strong>in</strong> one; it is a <strong>different way of investing</strong>, not a different product.</li>\n<li>A mutual fund is set up as a <strong>trust</strong>.</li>\n<li>Profits and losses belong <strong>only to unitholders</strong>; everyone else earns a <strong>fee or commission</strong>.</li>\n<li>MFs act as <strong>market stabiliser</strong>: in <strong>2022 and 2025</strong> MF inflows countered heavy <strong>FPI</strong> selling.</li>\n<li>Each separate pool of money = a <strong>scheme</strong>, with a pre-announced <strong>investment objective</strong>.</li>\n<li>Investment policy = <strong>asset allocation + investment style</strong>, disclosed in the <strong>SID</strong>.</li>\n<li>Face value = typically <strong>Rs 10</strong> (can differ, e.g. <strong>Rs 100</strong>); unit capital = <strong>units × face value</strong>.</li>\n<li>AUM = <strong>NAV × units outstanding</strong>; AMCs are ranked by <strong>AUM</strong>.</li>\n<li>Dividend option is now <strong>IDCW</strong> (Income Distribution cum Capital Withdrawal).</li>\n<li>NAV = <strong>net realisable value per unit</strong> if the scheme were liquidated.</li>\n<li><strong>Mark to market</strong> = valuing each security at market price, <strong>daily</strong>.</li>\n<li>Recurring expenses are charged as a <strong>% of AUM</strong> and <strong>lower the NAV</strong>.</li>\n<li>Diversification from as little as <strong>Rs 500</strong>.</li>\n<li>Transparency sources: <strong>SID, SAI, KIM + portfolio disclosures + NAV</strong>; available to <strong>prospective investors</strong> too.</li>\n<li><strong>Tax deferral</strong>: MFs don't pay tax on income; growth option defers tax.</li>\n<li><strong>ELSS</strong>: deduction up to <strong>Rs 1,50,000</strong> under <strong>Sec 80C</strong>, <strong>Old Tax Regime</strong> only.</li>\n<li>Systematic plans: <strong>SIP</strong> (invest), <strong>SWP</strong> (withdraw), <strong>STP</strong> (transfer).</li>\n<li>Limitations: <strong>no customisation</strong> (PMS gives it), <strong>choice overload</strong>, <strong>no control over costs</strong>, <strong>no guaranteed returns</strong>.</li>\n<li>SEBI <strong>categorisation</strong> was the answer to choice overload.</li>\n<li>MF is a <strong>pass-through vehicle</strong>; the fund manager controls only <strong>his own skill</strong>, not the market or securities.</li>\n<li><strong>Open-ended</strong>: buy/sell any time after NFO; <strong>unit capital keeps changing</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Close-ended</strong>: buy only in <strong>NFO</strong>; <strong>fixed maturity</strong>; <strong>compulsorily listed</strong> (except <strong>ELSS</strong>); <strong>unit capital stays fixed</strong>.</li>\n<li>Close-ended units usually trade at a <strong>discount to NAV</strong> (buyer has the bargaining power).</li>\n<li><strong>Interval fund</strong>: close-ended, opens at intervals; <strong>listed</strong>; transaction period <strong>min 2 days</strong>; interval period <strong>15 days</strong> (workbook wording).</li>\n<li><strong>ETFs</strong> trade all day at multiple prices, need a <strong>demat account</strong>, and track NAV closely; <strong>Silver ETF</strong> now allowed.</li>\n<li><strong>Active</strong>: higher cost, aims to beat the market. <strong>Passive/index</strong>: tracks index, <strong>low cost</strong>, no fund-manager choice.</li>\n<li><strong>MF Lite</strong>: <strong>Chapter X</strong> of the MF Regulations 2026; a lighter framework for <strong>passive</strong> schemes only (index funds, ETFs, FoFs).</li>\n<li>SEBI categorisation circular (<strong>2026</strong>): <strong>5 categories</strong>: Equity <strong>13</strong>, Debt <strong>17</strong>, Hybrid <strong>7</strong>, <strong>Life Cycle Funds</strong>, Other <strong>2</strong>.</li>\n<li>Market cap: <strong>Large 1–100</strong>, <strong>Mid 101–250</strong>, <strong>Small 251+</strong> by <strong>full market cap</strong>.</li>\n<li>Multi cap: equity <strong>≥75%</strong>, with <strong>≥25% each</strong> in large, mid, small.</li>\n<li>Large cap <strong>80%</strong>; Large &amp; Mid <strong>35% + 35%</strong>; Mid cap <strong>65%</strong>; Small cap <strong>65%</strong>.</li>\n<li>Dividend yield <strong>80%</strong>.</li>\n<li>Value <strong>and</strong> Contra are now <strong>separate</strong> categories, <strong>80%</strong> each; an AMC can offer <strong>both</strong> if portfolio overlap is <strong>≤50%</strong>.</li>\n<li>Focused: <strong>max 30 stocks</strong>, <strong>80%</strong> equity.</li>\n<li><strong>Sectoral</strong> and <strong>Thematic</strong> are <strong>separate</strong> categories, <strong>80%</strong> each; overlap with other sectoral/thematic and other equity schemes <strong>≤50%</strong> (<strong>except large cap</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>ELSS – Tax Saver Fund</strong>: <strong>80%</strong> + <strong>3-year lock-in</strong>; Flexi cap <strong>65%</strong>, any mix.</li>\n<li>Equity schemes may put the <strong>residual</strong> in money market, <strong>gold and silver</strong> instruments and <strong>InvITs</strong>.</li>\n<li>Overnight = <strong>1 day</strong>; can put <strong>≤5%</strong> in G-secs/T-bills (<strong>≤30 days</strong>) for margin.</li>\n<li>Liquid = <strong>up to 91 days</strong>; Money market = <strong>up to 1 year</strong>.</li>\n<li>New debt names (Macaulay duration): <strong>Ultra-Short-Term</strong> <strong>3–6 m</strong>; <strong>Ultra Short to Short Term</strong> (was low duration) <strong>6–12 m</strong>; <strong>Short Term</strong> <strong>1–3 y</strong>; <strong>Medium Term</strong> <strong>3–4 y</strong>; <strong>Medium to Long Term</strong> <strong>4–7 y</strong>; <strong>Long Term</strong> <strong>&gt;7 y</strong>; <strong>Dynamic Term</strong> = <strong>any</strong>.</li>\n<li>Corporate bond <strong>80% in AA+ and above</strong>; Credit risk <strong>65% in AA and below</strong> (AA+ excluded).</li>\n<li>Credit-risk scheme names can't stress only return (<strong>no \"high yield\", \"credit opportunities\"</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Banking and PSU Debt</strong> <strong>80%</strong>; Gilt <strong>80%</strong> G-secs; <strong>10-year constant maturity Gilt</strong> <strong>80%</strong>, duration <strong>= 10 yrs</strong>; <strong>Floating Interest Rates Fund</strong> <strong>65%</strong>.</li>\n<li>New <strong>Sectoral (debt) Fund</strong>: <strong>80%</strong> in one sector, <strong>only AA+ and above</strong>; sectors: <strong>Financial Services, Energy, Infrastructure, Housing, Real Estate</strong>.</li>\n<li>Conservative hybrid: debt <strong>75–90%</strong>, equity <strong>10–25%</strong>.</li>\n<li>Balanced hybrid: equity <strong>40–60%</strong>, <strong>no arbitrage</strong>. Aggressive hybrid: equity <strong>65–80%</strong>. Category is now \"Balanced Hybrid <strong>and</strong> Aggressive Hybrid\".</li>\n<li>Balanced Advantage = <strong>dynamic</strong> equity/debt.</li>\n<li>Multi asset: <strong>≥3 asset classes</strong>, <strong>≥10% each</strong>; <strong>foreign securities are not a separate class</strong>.</li>\n<li>Arbitrage: <strong>65%</strong> equity; debt only in <strong>G-secs under 1 year</strong> and <strong>G-sec repo</strong>.</li>\n<li>Equity savings: <strong>65%</strong> equity + <strong>10%</strong> debt; <strong>net equity 15–40%</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hybrid schemes may put the <strong>residual</strong> in <strong>InvITs</strong> (not arbitrage funds), <strong>ETCDs, Gold and Silver ETFs</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Life Cycle Fund</strong>: <strong>glide path</strong> across equity, debt, InvITs, ETCDs, gold and silver ETFs (equity falls, debt rises as maturity nears); tenure <strong>5–30 years</strong> in <strong>multiples of 5</strong>; max <strong>6</strong> open for subscription per MF at a time.</li>\n<li>Workbook illustration (15-year Life Cycle Fund), equity / debt / gold-silver-ETCD-InvIT: <strong>10–15 yrs to maturity 65–80 / 5–25 / 0–10</strong>; <strong>5–10 yrs 50–65 / 5–25</strong>; <strong>3–5 yrs 35–50 / 25–50</strong>; <strong>1–3 yrs 20–35 / 25–65</strong>; <strong>&lt;1 yr 5–20 / 25–65</strong>.</li>\n<li>Index/ETF <strong>95%</strong> in index securities; FoF <strong>95%</strong> in underlying fund.</li>\n<li>One scheme per category, <strong>except</strong>: index funds/ETFs on different indices, FoFs with different underlyings, sector/thematic on different sectors.</li>\n<li><strong>FMP</strong>: close-ended debt; portfolio matches maturity; returns clearer but <strong>not guaranteed</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Target Maturity Fund</strong>: matures on a set date (<strong>2–14 years</strong>), <strong>listed</strong>, reduces <strong>reinvestment risk</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Capital protection fund</strong>: close-ended hybrid; e.g. <strong>Rs 90 in debt grows to Rs 100</strong>, rest in <strong>equity derivatives</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>IDF</strong>: close-ended, <strong>≥90%</strong> in infra debt; IDF-MF sponsored by <strong>banks and NBFCs</strong>; IDF-NBFC by <strong>banks and IFCs</strong> only.</li>\n<li><strong>REMF</strong>: invests in real estate assets in cities named in the SEBI circular of <strong>2 May 2008</strong>; <strong>close-ended, listed</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>REITs/InvITs are not MF schemes</strong>. REIT assets <strong>≥Rs 500 cr</strong>, offer <strong>≥Rs 250 cr</strong>; InvIT offer <strong>≥Rs 250 cr</strong>, assets <strong>≥Rs 500 cr</strong>; minimum lot <strong>Rs 10,000–15,000</strong>, traded lot <strong>1 unit</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>ESG</strong> sits under <strong>thematic</strong> equity; 6 strategies: exclusion, integration, best-in-class, impact, sustainable objectives, transition.</li>\n<li><strong>Smart beta</strong>: index-based but <strong>not market-cap weighted</strong> (e.g. equal weight).</li>\n<li><strong>Quant fund</strong>: model-driven, <strong>removes the human element</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SIF</strong>: fills the gap <strong>between MF and PMS</strong>; minimum <strong>Rs 10 lakh</strong> across strategies <strong>of the same AMC</strong>.</li>\n<li>SIF equity: Long-Short <strong>≥80%</strong> equity; Ex-Top 100 <strong>≥65%</strong>; Sector Rotation <strong>≥80%</strong>, <strong>max 4 sectors</strong>; redemptions <strong>daily or less often</strong>.</li>\n<li>SIF debt (interval): Sectoral debt <strong>≥2 sectors</strong>, <strong>max 75%</strong> per sector; redemptions <strong>weekly or less often</strong>.</li>\n<li>SIF hybrid (interval): Hybrid Long-Short <strong>≥25% each</strong> in equity and debt; redemptions <strong>twice a week or less often</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Every SIF strategy: max 25% unhedged short exposure</strong>; only <strong>one strategy per category</strong>.</li>\n<li>Industry AUM: <strong>Rs 11.89 lakh cr</strong> (Mar 2015) to <strong>Rs 82.03 lakh cr</strong> (Feb 2026), <strong>19.3% p.a.</strong> over 11 years.</li>\n<li>Folios: <strong>4.8 cr</strong> (Mar 2010) to <strong>27.39 cr</strong> (Mar 2026).</li>\n<li>Monthly SIP flow: <strong>Rs 8,055 cr</strong> (Mar 2019) to <strong>Rs 32,087 cr</strong> (Mar 2026); SIP assets <strong>Rs 15.11 lakh cr</strong> (Mar 2026).</li>\n<li>MF share of household financial investment: <strong>10% → 14%</strong> (2016–18); bank deposits <strong>71% → 65%</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li>Unit capital = units x <strong>face value</strong>; AUM = units x <strong>NAV</strong>.</li>\n<li>Close-ended schemes are compulsorily listed <strong>except ELSS</strong>.</li>\n<li>Credit risk fund: 65% in <strong>AA and below</strong> (AA+ excluded); corporate bond fund: 80% in <strong>AA+ and above</strong>.</li>\n<li>Mid cap = <strong>101-250</strong>, not 101-200; small cap = <strong>251 onwards</strong>, by full market cap.</li>\n<li>Focused fund: max <strong>30</strong> stocks, not 20, with 80% equity.</li>\n<li>Large cap, dividend yield, value, contra, sectoral, thematic: <strong>80%</strong>; mid cap, small cap and flexi cap: <strong>65%</strong>.</li>\n<li>ELSS: <strong>80%</strong> equity, not 65%, with a <strong>3-year</strong> lock-in; 80C deduction in the <strong>Old</strong> Tax Regime only.</li>\n<li>Index funds, ETFs and FoFs: <strong>95%</strong>, not 80%.</li>\n<li>Ultra-Short-Term = <strong>3-6 months</strong>; Ultra Short to Short Term (old low duration) = <strong>6-12 months</strong>.</li>\n<li>Balanced Advantage = <strong>dynamic</strong> equity/debt; Balanced Hybrid = 40-60% equity with <strong>no arbitrage</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-b (NAV), 2-c (economies of scale), 3-c (close-ended), 4-c (no limit if overlap &lt;50%), 5-b (equity down, debt up).</p>"},{"id":"ch3","n":3,"title":"Legal Structure of Mutual Funds","marks":4,"html":"<ul>\n<li>Governing regulation: <strong>SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 2026</strong>.</li>\n<li>MF definition: a fund set up as a <strong>trust</strong>, <strong>granted registration under Regulation 3</strong>, raising money by selling <strong>units</strong> to the public under <strong>schemes</strong>, to invest in securities, money market, <strong>gold, silver</strong> and other assets SEBI specifies.</li>\n<li>Trust is governed by the <strong>Indian Trusts Act, 1882</strong>; trust deed registered under the <strong>Indian Registration Act, 1908</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sponsor</strong> creates the trust; <strong>beneficiaries = unitholders</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trust deed</strong> is executed between <strong>sponsor and trustees</strong>.</li>\n<li>Trustees can be individuals (<strong>Board of Trustees</strong>) or a <strong>trustee company</strong> (acts through its Board of Directors).</li>\n<li><strong>AMC</strong> is appointed by the <strong>sponsor or trustees</strong>, with <strong>SEBI approval</strong>.</li>\n<li>Trustees sign the <strong>Investment Management Agreement</strong> with the AMC.</li>\n<li><strong>Custodian</strong> is appointed by the <strong>trustees</strong>; <strong>RTA</strong> by the <strong>AMC</strong>; <strong>collecting banker</strong> by the <strong>AMC</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sponsor applies</strong> to SEBI for MF registration and invests in the <strong>AMC's capital</strong>.</li>\n<li>Sponsor eligibility: <strong>5 years</strong> in financial services; <strong>positive net worth</strong> in each of the last <strong>5 years</strong>; <strong>net profit</strong> in each of the last <strong>5 years</strong>; <strong>average profit ≥ Rs 10 crore</strong> over 5 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Liquid net worth</strong> must exceed the sponsor's proposed <strong>capital contribution</strong> to the AMC.</li>\n<li>Trustee eligibility (<strong>Regulation 11</strong>): <strong>fit and proper</strong> (Schedule II, Intermediaries Regulations 2008); <strong>not a director, officer or employee of an AMC</strong>; <strong>not trustee of another MF</strong>; furnishes information SEBI asks for.</li>\n<li>Trustee appointment needs <strong>prior SEBI approval</strong>.</li>\n<li>At least <strong>two-thirds</strong> of the trustee board = <strong>independent directors</strong> (not associates of the sponsor, AMC or trustee company).</li>\n<li>Trustee duties (6 heads): <strong>appoint/terminate the AMC</strong>, <strong>conflicts of interest and broker empanelment</strong>, <strong>regulatory compliance</strong>, <strong>oversight for investor interests</strong>, <strong>additional oversight</strong>, due diligence on matters SEBI specifies.</li>\n<li>AMC directors: <strong>professional experience</strong> in finance; directors and key personnel must be <strong>fit and proper</strong>.</li>\n<li>At least <strong>50%</strong> of AMC directors must <strong>not be associates</strong> of the sponsor, its subsidiaries or the trustees.</li>\n<li>AMC directors (<strong>incl. Chairman</strong>) can't sit on the board of <strong>any MF's trustee company</strong>; no one can be a director of <strong>more than one AMC</strong>.</li>\n<li>AMC director appointment needs <strong>prior trustee approval</strong>.</li>\n<li>AMC <strong>net worth ≥ Rs 50 crore</strong> (from <strong>1 Jan 2024</strong>); maintained <strong>continuously</strong>; <strong>sponsor's responsibility</strong>.</li>\n<li>Change of AMC control needs <strong>prior approval of trustees and SEBI</strong>.</li>\n<li>On change of control: letter to each unitholder, ad in <strong>1 English national daily + 1 regional-language paper</strong>, and <strong>exit at NAV with no exit load for ≥30 calendar days</strong>.</li>\n<li>AMC can be removed by <strong>majority of trustees</strong> or <strong>75% of unitholders</strong>; any change needs <strong>SEBI + unitholder approval</strong>.</li>\n<li>Custodian registers under <strong>SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996</strong>.</li>\n<li>Custodian can't be one where the sponsor holds <strong>≥50% voting rights</strong> or <strong>≥50% of directors</strong>, unless conditions are met.</li>\n<li><strong>Custodian tracks corporate actions</strong> (dividends, bonus, rights) and settles trades.</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance Officer</strong> signs the <strong>due-diligence certificate</strong> on new scheme documents; reports to the <strong>AMC head</strong>; flags non-compliance <strong>directly to trustees</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fund management sub-teams: <strong>analysts</strong> (find ideas), <strong>fund managers</strong> (own scheme performance), <strong>dealers</strong> (place orders).</li>\n<li><strong>Fund accounting</strong> team calculates NAV <strong>daily</strong>; the AMC's <strong>accounts</strong> team is separate.</li>\n<li>SEBI <strong>Risk Management Framework</strong>: dedicated risk officers for <strong>investment, compliance, operational, cyber</strong> risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Fund accountant</strong>: <strong>no SEBI registration</strong> needed; can be in-house.</li>\n<li><strong>RTA</strong>: <strong>must register with SEBI</strong>, but appointing one is <strong>optional</strong>; its offices act as <strong>ISCs</strong> (Investor Service Centres).</li>\n<li><strong>Scheme auditor</strong>: appointed by <strong>trustees</strong>, must <strong>differ</strong> from the <strong>AMC auditor</strong> (appointed by the AMC).</li>\n<li>MFD needs <strong>NISM V-A + AMFI registration</strong>; the limited-scheme cadre needs <strong>NISM V-B</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>KRAs</strong>: SEBI regulations in <strong>2011</strong>; one KYC across the securities market, under <strong>PMLA</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fair valuation of non-traded debt uses <strong>≥2 valuation agencies</strong>: <strong>CRISIL and ICRA</strong>, appointed by AMFI; AMCs use their <strong>valuation level</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>FMP</strong>: may disclose <strong>proposed rating profile</strong> before investing; can then invest only <strong>at that rating or safer</strong>.</li>\n<li>Depositories: <strong>NSDL and CDSL</strong>; investors deal through <strong>DPs</strong> (like bank branches).</li>\n<li><strong>ETF units must be in demat</strong>.</li>\n<li>Exchange platforms: <strong>BSE StAR MF</strong> and <strong>NSE NMF-II</strong>; <strong>MF Utilities</strong> is an <strong>AMFI industry</strong> platform.</li>\n<li><strong>Execution Only Platform</strong> = only for <strong>direct plans</strong>; RIA and broker platforms for their own clients are <strong>not EOPs</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>EOP Category 1</strong>: registers with <strong>AMFI</strong>, body corporate, <strong>agent of AMCs</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>EOP Category 2</strong>: registers as a <strong>stock broker</strong> in the exchange EOP segment, <strong>agent of investor</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>AMFI</strong> = association of all AMCs; issues <strong>ARN</strong>; can <strong>cancel ARN</strong> for code violations.</li>\n<li><strong>AMFI is neither a regulator nor an SRO</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li>Custodian is appointed by the <strong>trustees</strong>, not the AMC; the RTA and collecting banker are appointed by the <strong>AMC</strong>.</li>\n<li>Trust deed is between the <strong>sponsor and trustees</strong>; the Investment Management Agreement is between the <strong>trustees and AMC</strong>.</li>\n<li>Sponsor eligibility uses <strong>5 years</strong> throughout (experience, net worth, profit), with average profit <strong>≥ Rs 10 crore</strong>.</li>\n<li>Trustee board: <strong>two-thirds</strong> independent; AMC board: <strong>50%</strong> non-associate.</li>\n<li>AMC net worth <strong>≥ Rs 50 crore</strong>, maintained continuously, is the <strong>sponsor's</strong> responsibility.</li>\n<li>AMC removal: <strong>majority of trustees</strong> or <strong>75% of unitholders</strong>; any change needs SEBI + unitholder approval.</li>\n<li>Fund accountant needs <strong>no SEBI registration</strong>; the RTA <strong>must register</strong> with SEBI but appointing one is <strong>optional</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>AMFI</strong> is neither a regulator nor an SRO; CRISIL and ICRA are appointed by <strong>AMFI</strong>, not SEBI.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-c (unitholders), 2-b (custodian), 3-a (association of AMCs), 4-c (CRISIL and ICRA), 5-a (AMC).</p>"},{"id":"ch4","n":4,"title":"Legal and Regulatory Framework","marks":10,"html":"<h3>Regulators and SEBI</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>4 regulators: <strong>RBI</strong> (banks, money market), <strong>SEBI</strong> (securities), <strong>IRDAI</strong> (insurance), <strong>PFRDA</strong> (pension); all under <strong>Ministry of Finance</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SEBI regulates mutual funds</strong>; <strong>AMFI does not</strong>. MF regulations first issued in <strong>1996</strong>, now replaced by the <strong>MF Regulations 2026</strong>.</li>\n<li>\"Securities\" = transferable, tradeable instruments, defined in <strong>Sec 2(h) of the SCRA, 1956</strong>.</li>\n<li>SEBI preamble: <strong>protect investors, develop and regulate</strong> the securities market.</li>\n<li>MF schemes are benchmarked to a <strong>Total Return Index (TRI)</strong>, which includes dividends and interest, not a <strong>Price Return Index</strong>.</li>\n<li>Debt schemes carry a <strong>Potential Risk Class (PRC) matrix</strong>: <strong>Macaulay duration</strong> × <strong>Credit Risk Value</strong>; effective <strong>1 Dec 2021</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Segregated portfolio</strong> separates distressed assets after a <strong>credit event</strong>; introduced <strong>after</strong> the <strong>2018</strong> credit crisis.</li>\n<li><strong>2008</strong> crisis reforms: <strong>no premature redemption in FMPs</strong>; <strong>\"liquid plus\"</strong> name banned.</li>\n<li>RBI rules also apply to MFs for <strong>money market, overseas investment, forex</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Investment restrictions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Regulation 39</strong>: an MF may invest <strong>only</strong> in securities, money market instruments, <strong>privately placed debentures</strong>, <strong>securitised debt</strong> (asset or mortgage backed), <strong>gold</strong>, <strong>silver</strong> and anything else SEBI specifies.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulation 41</strong>: all investments follow the <strong>Sixth Schedule</strong> (except <strong>gold and silver ETFs</strong>); settle <strong>only in demat</strong>; <strong>no carry-forward</strong> deals except as SEBI allows; derivatives and <strong>short selling</strong> only on a <strong>recognised stock exchange</strong>; <strong>inter-scheme transfers</strong> only on SEBI's conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Gold/silver ETFs</strong>: invest <strong>only in gold/silver</strong> (or related instruments), except for <strong>redemption liquidity</strong>; may use <strong>exchange-traded commodity derivatives</strong>; idle money in <strong>short-term bank deposits</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sixth Schedule</strong>: equity and debt only if <strong>listed or to be listed</strong>, except <strong>unlisted G-secs and money market instruments (not CPs)</strong> and <strong>unlisted NCDs</strong> as SEBI allows; <strong>unrated debt</strong> on SEBI's conditions.</li>\n<li>A scheme may invest in <strong>another MF scheme</strong> with <strong>no fees</strong> (not for FoFs); <strong>no scheme can invest in a FoF</strong>; a FoF <strong>can't invest in another FoF</strong> and holds <strong>only MF units</strong> (plus liquidity).</li>\n<li>Trades on <strong>delivery basis</strong> only (short selling and derivatives allowed under SEBI's framework); securities held <strong>in the MF's name</strong> for the scheme.</li>\n<li>Money awaiting deployment can sit in <strong>short-term deposits of scheduled commercial banks</strong>; all investments follow SEBI's <strong>prudential limits</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SIF</strong> (<strong>Regulation 51</strong>): same permitted instruments as MFs (Reg 39), and the <strong>Sixth Schedule</strong> restrictions apply.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Advertisement code</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No testimonials, no rankings, no celebrities, no projections</strong>; no unfair comparisons.</li>\n<li>Standard warning: \"<strong>Mutual Fund investments are subject to market risks, read all scheme related documents carefully.</strong>\" <strong>No words added or removed</strong>.</li>\n<li>Audio-visual: the <strong>14-word</strong> warning, visual + voice-over, <strong>at least 5 seconds</strong>.</li>\n<li>Vernacular ads carry the warning <strong>in the vernacular</strong>.</li>\n<li>Ad rules: <strong>Fifth Schedule</strong> of the MF Regulations 2026.</li>\n<li>IDCW ads: <strong>Rs per unit + face value + prevailing NAV</strong>; state <strong>just below the payout figure</strong> that NAV will <strong>fall by the payout and statutory levy</strong>.</li>\n<li>Overnight, liquid, money market: <strong>simple annualised</strong> yield allowed if data for <strong>7, 15 and 30 days</strong> exists.</li>\n<li>Scheme <strong>&gt;3 years</strong>: CAGR for <strong>1, 3, 5 years and since inception</strong> (since <strong>allotment date</strong>) + <strong>point-to-point on Rs 10,000</strong>; say <strong>regular or direct</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>&lt;6 months</strong>: <strong>no</strong> performance; <strong>6–12 months</strong>: <strong>simple annualised</strong> 6-month return.</li>\n<li>Additional benchmark: equity, balanced, aggressive, BAF, multi-asset, retirement, children's = <strong>Sensex/Nifty</strong>; debt ≤1 yr + <strong>arbitrage</strong> = <strong>1-yr T-bill</strong>; other debt, <strong>conservative hybrid, equity savings</strong> = <strong>10-yr GoI</strong>.</li>\n<li>Also show all schemes of the <strong>same fund manager</strong> (CAGR 1/3/5 yrs); <strong>&gt;6 schemes</strong>: count + <strong>top 3 and bottom 3</strong>.</li>\n<li>Celebrity endorsement: <strong>industry level only</strong>, <strong>prior SEBI approval</strong>, paid from the <strong>investor education</strong> pool; <strong>not</strong> for a scheme or AMC brand.</li>\n<li><strong>SIF</strong>: needs a <strong>distinct brand, logo, website/page</strong>; MF brand allowed for <strong>5 years</strong> from SEBI approval, with phrases like <strong>\"brought to you by\"</strong>; the MF brand's font must be <strong>no bigger</strong> than the SIF's; MF ad rules apply.</li>\n<li>Unverified market news: forward only after the <strong>Compliance Officer's</strong> approval.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Investor rights</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Proportionate <strong>beneficial ownership</strong>; can ask for a <strong>unit certificate</strong> or <strong>demat</strong>; demat units <strong>freely transferable</strong> (<strong>except ELSS</strong> in lock-in).</li>\n<li>Switch distributor or go direct by <strong>written request</strong>; <strong>no NOC</strong> needed.</li>\n<li>Can inspect: <strong>Trust Deed, IMA, Custodial agreement, RTA agreement, AMC Memorandum &amp; Articles</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Up to 10 nominees</strong>; no % given = <strong>equal split</strong>.</li>\n<li>Can <strong>pledge</strong> units.</li>\n<li>Complaint status goes in the <strong>annual report</strong>, MF site and <strong>AMFI site</strong>; pending complaints can <strong>block new scheme launches</strong>.</li>\n<li>Change in <strong>fundamental attributes</strong> (or fees): <strong>written communication</strong> to each unitholder (<strong>email/SMS allowed</strong>) + details on the <strong>AMC website</strong> + <strong>exit at NAV with no load</strong>, window <strong>≥30 days</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>75% of unitholders</strong> can <strong>remove the AMC</strong> or <strong>wind up</strong> a scheme.</li>\n<li>Sue the <strong>trustees</strong> for breach of trust, <strong>not the trust</strong> (a notional entity).</li>\n<li><strong>Caveat emptor</strong> applies; a <strong>prospective investor</strong> has no such rights.</li>\n<li>Unclaimed money <strong>within 3 yrs</strong>: paid at <strong>prevailing NAV</strong>; <strong>after 3 yrs</strong>: at <strong>NAV at end of year 3</strong>.</li>\n<li>Unclaimed amounts go to a <strong>separate liquid plan</strong>; max fee <strong>0.50% p.a.</strong>; <strong>no exit load</strong>.</li>\n<li>Debt trades disclosed <strong>daily</strong>, with a <strong>15-day lag</strong>.</li>\n<li>Illiquid security recovered later: substantial and <strong>within 2 yrs</strong> goes to <strong>old investors</strong>; otherwise to the <strong>Investor Education Fund</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Grievances and codes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>AMC resolves complaints within <strong>21 calendar days</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SCORES</strong> = SEBI's online complaints system; physical complaints get <strong>scanned and uploaded</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cyber framework <strong>CSCRF</strong> goals (from <strong>CERT-In</strong>): <strong>Anticipate, Withstand, Contain, Recover, Evolve</strong>.</li>\n<li>AMCs do <strong>due diligence</strong> on distributors; <strong>AMFI</strong> often runs a <strong>common</strong> process.</li>\n<li><strong>Fourth Schedule</strong> = SEBI code of conduct for <strong>AMCs and trustees</strong>; <strong>AMFI Code of Ethics (ACE)</strong> sets <strong>higher, more explicit</strong> standards.</li>\n<li><strong>AGNI</strong> = AMFI Guidelines &amp; Norms for <strong>Intermediaries</strong>.</li>\n<li>Breach process: explanation within <strong>3 weeks</strong> → <strong>warning letter</strong> → <strong>2nd proven violation = ARN cancelled</strong>, all AMCs told; <strong>appeal to AMFI</strong> allowed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>MFD code (Appendix 3)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>MFD acts as a <strong>fiduciary</strong>: <strong>investor's interest is paramount</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>No rebates or pass-back</strong>, no gifts or inducements, <strong>no churning</strong>, <strong>no splitting applications</strong>.</li>\n<li>Disclose <strong>all commissions</strong> across competing schemes and <strong>group/affiliate conflicts</strong>.</li>\n<li>MFDs <strong>cannot deal in direct plans</strong>; platforms must say <strong>regular plan with commission</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>No indicative portfolio, yield or assured returns</strong>.</li>\n<li>Never enter the <strong>MFD's own contact details</strong> on a client form; always add the <strong>EUIN</strong>.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>AMC-provided marketing material</strong> only; no AMC logo without <strong>written approval</strong>.</li>\n<li>Suitability: ask about <strong>financial status, experience, objectives</strong>.</li>\n<li>Don't share client data with <strong>group companies for cross-selling</strong>.</li>\n<li>Sub-distributors must hold a <strong>valid ARN</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Refund clawback</strong> commissions to AMCs.</li>\n<li>Report a Representative's <strong>moral turpitude or arrest</strong> to the <strong>AMC and AMFI</strong> immediately.</li>\n<li>Can't call yourself <strong>adviser, wealth manager or consultant</strong> unless a <strong>SEBI RIA</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tagline <strong>\"AMFI-registered Mutual Fund Distributor\"</strong>, font <strong>≥12</strong> in print.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li>Ad rules are in the <strong>Fifth Schedule</strong>; code of conduct for AMCs and trustees is the <strong>Fourth</strong>; investment restrictions are the <strong>Sixth</strong>.</li>\n<li>AMC resolves complaints within <strong>21 calendar days</strong>, not 30.</li>\n<li>Unclaimed money: within 3 years at <strong>prevailing NAV</strong>; after 3 years at the <strong>NAV at the end of year 3</strong>; liquid plan fee cap <strong>0.50%</strong>, not 1%.</li>\n<li>Celebrity endorsement is <strong>industry-level only</strong>, with prior SEBI approval, paid from the <strong>investor education</strong> pool; never for a scheme or AMC brand.</li>\n<li>Sue the <strong>trustees</strong> for breach of trust, not the trust (a notional entity).</li>\n<li>Ad performance: under 6 months <strong>none</strong>; 6 to 12 months <strong>simple annualised</strong> 6-month return, not CAGR.</li>\n<li>Additional benchmark: arbitrage and debt up to 1 year = <strong>1-yr T-bill</strong>; conservative hybrid and equity savings = <strong>10-yr GoI</strong>, not Sensex/Nifty.</li>\n<li>AGNI is for <strong>intermediaries</strong>; the Fourth Schedule code is for <strong>AMCs and trustees</strong>.</li>\n<li>Gold and silver ETFs are <strong>exempt</strong> from the Sixth Schedule; every other investment follows it.</li>\n<li>2008 reform banned the name <strong>\"liquid plus\"</strong>, not \"liquid\"; segregated portfolios came after the <strong>2018</strong> crisis; PRC uses <strong>Macaulay</strong> duration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-a (SEBI), 2-a (True), 3-a (industry-level celebrity), 4-d (ten).</p>"},{"id":"ch5","n":5,"title":"Scheme Related Information","marks":10,"html":"<h3>Mandatory documents</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scheme related documents</strong> = <strong>SID + SAI</strong> (+ <strong>KIM</strong>); the standard warning refers to these.</li>\n<li><strong>Caveat emptor</strong>: the investor is <strong>presumed to have read</strong> them; signing the form is <strong>legal acceptance</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SID</strong> = details of <strong>one scheme</strong>; <strong>SAI</strong> = statutory info on the <strong>MF/AMC</strong>, <strong>one SAI for all schemes</strong>; legally <strong>SAI is part of the SID</strong>.</li>\n<li>Format <strong>prescribed by SEBI</strong>; content in the <strong>same sequence</strong>; MF may add other <strong>material</strong> disclosures.</li>\n<li>SEBI <strong>does not approve</strong>, it gives <strong>observations</strong>; documents are <strong>vetted</strong>, not approved.</li>\n<li><strong>Drafts</strong> on <strong>SEBI's</strong> website; <strong>final</strong> documents on <strong>AMFI's</strong> website and the MF's own site.</li>\n<li>Offer = documents; <strong>acceptance</strong> = signed form + payment; <strong>contract completes</strong> on unit allotment.</li>\n<li>SID cover page: name, type (open/close/interval), <strong>face value</strong>, NFO dates, AMC and trustee details.</li>\n<li>Cover disclaimer: filed with SEBI with an AMC <strong>Due Diligence Certificate</strong>; <strong>SEBI has not approved or certified</strong> it.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk-o-meter and suitability statement</strong> sit on the <strong>front page of the SID</strong>.</li>\n<li>Standard risk: <strong>sponsor not liable</strong> beyond its <strong>initial contribution</strong>; <strong>past performance not indicative</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Eligible and ineligible investors</strong>: SID's <strong>NFO and Ongoing Offer</strong> section.</li>\n<li>Investment objective can be <strong>primary + secondary</strong>; also shown in <strong>Highlights</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>How to apply</strong> (PAN, KYC, payment modes, third-party rules, cut-off, minors, NRIs): <strong>SAI</strong>, read with SID.</li>\n<li><strong>Modes of transaction</strong>: SAI's <strong>\"How to Apply\"</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fees</strong> hit the <strong>NAV</strong>; <strong>loads</strong> are paid <strong>by the investor</strong> at exit.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Fundamental attributes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>(i) <strong>Type of scheme</strong>; (ii) <strong>investment objective</strong> + <strong>min/max asset allocation</strong>; (iii) <strong>terms of issue</strong>: liquidity, <strong>aggregate fees and expenses</strong>, <strong>any safety net or guarantee</strong>.</li>\n<li>To change them (<strong>Regulation 22(9)(c)</strong>): <strong>written communication</strong> to each unitholder (<strong>email/SMS allowed</strong>) + details on the <strong>AMC website</strong> + <strong>exit at prevailing NAV with no exit load</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>SAI and KIM</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SAI</strong> covers constituents (sponsor, AMC, trustee, custodian, RTA, auditor, fund accountant if outsourced, bankers), <strong>condensed financials</strong> for schemes of the <strong>last 3 FYs</strong>, <strong>valuation norms</strong>, nomination, transmission, minors, pledge, tax, grievances.</li>\n<li>Investors can ask for a <strong>printed SAI</strong>; all SAIs on <strong>AMFI's</strong> site.</li>\n<li><strong>KIM</strong> = summary of <strong>SID + SAI</strong>; <strong>every application form must carry a KIM</strong>.</li>\n<li>KIM shows performance over <strong>1, 3, 5 years and since inception</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Addendum</strong> = interim change; it is <strong>part of the documents</strong> and <strong>must go with the KIM</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Update timelines</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SID</strong>: first update within <strong>6 months</strong> of the half-year of launch; then within <strong>1 month of each half-year</strong> (<strong>Sept and March</strong> data).</li>\n<li>Change in fundamental attributes: <strong>addendum immediately</strong>; SID revised <strong>after the exit window</strong> (≥30 days); public notice.</li>\n<li>Other changes: <strong>addendum</strong> on website and to <strong>distributors and ISCs</strong>, attached to KIM/SID stock; <strong>account statements show the load structure</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SAI</strong>: within <strong>3 months of FY end</strong>; material changes <strong>ongoing</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>KIM</strong>: at least <strong>once every half-year</strong>, within <strong>1 month</strong>; filed with SEBI <strong>electronically</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Other disclosures</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Current value = <strong>units × NAV</strong>.</li>\n<li>NAV <strong>every business day</strong>, on the <strong>AMC and AMFI</strong> sites.</li>\n<li><strong>Liquid and overnight</strong> NAVs also declared for <strong>Sundays and holidays</strong>; close-ended NAV <strong>at least weekly</strong>.</li>\n<li>Trustees and AMCs ensure every expense is <strong>identified and disclosed</strong> in the offer document and within the Regulations.</li>\n<li><strong>TER</strong> disclosed <strong>daily</strong> on the AMC and AMFI sites (<strong>all schemes except IDFs</strong>), in <strong>Format 7E</strong>, downloadable and <strong>machine-readable</strong>.</li>\n<li>Any expense item <strong>&gt;10% of total expenditure</strong> is disclosed in the scheme accounts.</li>\n<li><strong>TER = Base Expense Ratio (BER) + brokerage + transaction cost + statutory levies (incl. GST)</strong>; BER under <strong>Regulation 66(7)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Direct plan BER is <strong>lower</strong> (no distribution cost, <strong>no commission</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Any change in BER</strong>: email/SMS and website <strong>≥3 working days before</strong> it takes effect (effective 8 Jan 2026 = notice by <strong>2 Jan</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Scheme dashboard</strong> on the AMC site: AUM, TER, fund manager, benchmark, exit load, portfolio, performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk-o-meter</strong>: <strong>6 levels</strong>: Low (<strong>Irish Green</strong>), Low to Moderate (<strong>Chartreuse</strong>), Moderate (<strong>Neon Yellow</strong>), Moderately High (<strong>Caramel</strong>), High (<strong>Dark Orange</strong>), Very High (<strong>Red</strong>).</li>\n<li>Risk-o-meter reviewed <strong>monthly</strong>; change = <strong>notice-cum-addendum + email/SMS</strong>; show the <strong>scheme and benchmark</strong> risk-o-meter together.</li>\n<li>Commodity risk score from <strong>annualised volatility</strong>, reviewed <strong>quarterly</strong> on <strong>15 years</strong> of prices: <strong>&lt;10% = 3</strong>, <strong>10–15% = 4</strong>, <strong>15–20% = 5</strong>, <strong>&gt;20% = 6</strong>.</li>\n<li>Risk-o-meter is only the <strong>first step</strong>, not the sole basis for decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>SIF Risk-Band</strong>: <strong>5 levels</strong>; set at <strong>NFO</strong>; reviewed <strong>monthly</strong>; published within <strong>10 days</strong>; <strong>31 March</strong> level + number of changes on AMC and AMFI sites.</li>\n<li><strong>Portfolio</strong>: <strong>monthly and half-yearly</strong>, with <strong>ISIN</strong>, within <strong>10 days</strong>, on AMC and AMFI sites.</li>\n<li><strong>Debt schemes</strong>: portfolio <strong>fortnightly</strong>, within <strong>5 days</strong>, <strong>plus yield</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SIF portfolio</strong>: <strong>every alternate month</strong> (May, Jul, Sep, Nov, Jan, Mar), within <strong>10 days</strong>; SIF <strong>ISID</strong> must include <strong>scenario analysis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Unaudited half-yearly results</strong> hosted on the website within <strong>1 month</strong> of 31 March / 30 Sept, in SEBI's format; unitholders told by <strong>written communication (email/SMS)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Annual report</strong> within <strong>4 months</strong> of FY end; ad in <strong>English + Hindi</strong> national dailies; <strong>functional link</strong> to scheme-wise annual reports on the website.</li>\n<li><strong>Factsheet</strong> is <strong>not mandatory</strong> (voluntary); publishing it <strong>monthly</strong> is an <strong>AMFI best practice</strong>; SEBI disclosure rules still apply; shows PE, beta, SD, Sharpe, rating profile, maturity, duration.</li>\n<li>MFs may use derivatives <strong>to hedge</strong>, but <strong>can't take open derivative positions</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li>SEBI gives <strong>observations</strong> on the SID; it does <strong>not approve</strong> or certify it (documents are vetted, not approved).</li>\n<li><strong>One SAI</strong> serves all schemes of a fund; the <strong>SID</strong> is per scheme; legally the SAI is part of the SID.</li>\n<li>Drafts go on <strong>SEBI's</strong> website; final documents on <strong>AMFI's</strong> website and the MF's own site.</li>\n<li>Risk-o-meter is reviewed <strong>monthly</strong>; commodity risk score is reviewed <strong>quarterly</strong> on <strong>15 years</strong> of prices.</li>\n<li>SAI update: within <strong>3 months</strong> of FY end; SID and KIM: within <strong>1 month</strong> of each half-year.</li>\n<li>Annual report within <strong>4 months</strong> of FY end; unaudited half-yearly results within <strong>1 month</strong>.</li>\n<li>Debt portfolio: <strong>fortnightly, within 5 days</strong>, plus yield; all schemes: monthly and half-yearly within <strong>10 days</strong>.</li>\n<li>Low = <strong>Irish Green</strong>, Low to Moderate = <strong>Chartreuse</strong>, Moderate = Neon Yellow, Moderately High = <strong>Caramel</strong>, High = Dark Orange, Very High = Red.</li>\n<li>Factsheet is <strong>voluntary</strong>; monthly publication is an <strong>AMFI best practice</strong>, not a SEBI mandate.</li>\n<li><strong>Fees</strong> hit the NAV; <strong>loads</strong> are paid by the investor at exit. BER change needs <strong>3 working days'</strong> notice; BER sits under <strong>Regulation 66(7)</strong>, not 22(9)(c).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-d (SID + SAI), 2-c (SEBI format), 3-b (six months), 4-b (AMC), 5-a (factsheet).</p>"},{"id":"ch6","n":6,"title":"Fund Distribution and Channel Management","marks":6,"html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Distributor</strong> analyses the <strong>investor</strong>; <strong>fund manager</strong> analyses the <strong>market</strong>.</li>\n<li>Channels: individuals, banks, national/regional distributors, NBFCs, brokers, <strong>post offices</strong>, direct, online/<strong>e-commerce</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>LIC and UTI</strong> built the <strong>individual agent</strong> force.</li>\n<li>Employee model = <strong>fixed costs</strong> (salary); sub-agent model = <strong>variable costs</strong> (commission).</li>\n<li><strong>Multinational banks</strong> entered MF distribution <strong>first</strong>; PSU banks give <strong>non-urban reach</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Distribution companies and banks</strong> = <strong>institutional distributors</strong>.</li>\n<li>Paper forms go to <strong>OPOAs</strong> (Official Points of Acceptance).</li>\n<li>Exchange platforms: <strong>NSE NMF-II</strong>, <strong>BSE StAR MF</strong>; only <strong>AMFI-registered</strong> trading members who have signed up with the MF.</li>\n<li><strong>DPs can only process redemptions</strong> on exchange platforms; eligible open-ended schemes are <strong>not listed</strong>.</li>\n<li>AMFI-registered distributors can take <strong>limited purpose membership</strong> of an exchange.</li>\n<li>Exchanges are an <strong>order-routing</strong> system: they <strong>don't replace RTAs</strong> and give <strong>no settlement guarantee</strong> (the <strong>AMC</strong> is responsible); exchange grievance redressal still applies.</li>\n<li>Investors may <strong>directly</strong> use exchange infrastructure (SEBI circular, <strong>26 Feb 2020</strong>).</li>\n<li>SIP, STP, SWP and switch all work on exchanges; units credited to <strong>demat</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>MF Utilities</strong>: <strong>CAN</strong> (Common Account Number) needs <strong>KYC</strong>; <strong>one form, one payment</strong> for multiple schemes; <strong>MFU bank/nomination details override folio details</strong>; an investor can have <strong>multiple CANs</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>RTA interoperable platform</strong> live since <strong>31 Dec 2021</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>MFCentral</strong>: single-window portal built by <strong>CAMS and KFintech</strong> under SEBI mandate: <strong>consolidated view</strong> of demat and non-demat folios, <strong>service requests</strong> (bank, email/mobile, KYC, nominee), <strong>transactions</strong> (lump sum, SIP, redemption, switch), folio consolidation, <strong>CAS</strong>, unclaimed amounts, <strong>family portfolio</strong> tracking.</li>\n<li>New-age platforms (<strong>Groww, Kuvera, Paytm Money, Coin</strong>) sell <strong>direct plans</strong> too.</li>\n<li>Distributor must hold <strong>NISM V-A</strong>; exemption via <strong>CPE</strong> if <strong>age ≥50</strong> or <strong>≥10 yrs</strong> experience <strong>as of 31 May 2010</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>KYD</strong>: PAN + address proof at <strong>CAMS PoS</strong>, <strong>right index finger</strong> biometric; send the acknowledgement to all AMCs.</li>\n<li>New cadre (V-B) <strong>exempt from KYD biometrics</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>ARN</strong> minimum age <strong>18</strong>; employees get an <strong>EUIN</strong> under the firm's ARN (<strong>no separate ARN</strong>).</li>\n<li>ARN/EUIN applications <strong>online only</strong> from <strong>1 Oct 2024</strong>.</li>\n<li>Empanelment is <strong>compulsory</strong> to earn commission; <strong>no standard form</strong>; AMC can <strong>terminate anytime</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Full trail model only</strong> (SEBI <strong>Master Circular, 20 March 2026</strong>): <strong>no upfront</strong>, <strong>no upfronting of trail</strong>, <strong>monetary only</strong>; per <strong>Regulation 66(6)</strong> of the MF Regulations 2026 the <strong>AMC pays</strong> distribution costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Transaction charges withdrawn</strong> (SEBI, <strong>Aug 2025</strong>); earlier applied to subscriptions <strong>&gt;Rs 10,000</strong>.</li>\n<li>Trail is calculated on <strong>daily net assets</strong>, paid <strong>monthly or quarterly</strong>, for <strong>as long as the money stays</strong>.</li>\n<li>Daily trail = <strong>AUM × rate ÷ 365</strong>; Rs 10 lakh at 1% = <strong>Rs 27.40/day</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>No commission on self-investment</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Additional commission</strong> for new investors (SEBI, <strong>Nov 2025</strong>): <strong>new PAN</strong> from <strong>B-30</strong> cities, or <strong>new women</strong> investors from <strong>Top 30 and B-30</strong>.</li>\n<li>Lump sum: <strong>1%</strong> of the first application, <strong>max Rs 2,000</strong>, if the investor stays <strong>≥1 year</strong>. SIP: <strong>1%</strong> of the <strong>first year's</strong> investment, <strong>max Rs 2,000</strong>.</li>\n<li>Paid from the <strong>2 bps</strong> set aside for <strong>investor education</strong>, with <strong>claw back</strong>; it is <strong>on top of</strong> trail; <strong>no dual incentive</strong> for the same investor.</li>\n<li><strong>GST</strong> (from <strong>July 2017</strong>) threshold <strong>Rs 20 lakh</strong>; a registered distributor invoices and pays GST; for <strong>unregistered</strong> suppliers the <strong>registered recipient</strong> pays under <strong>reverse charge</strong> (Sec 9(4) CGST, 5(4) IGST).</li>\n<li>Commission disclosure and <strong>due diligence</strong> trigger (<strong>any one</strong> of): <strong>&gt;20 locations</strong>, <strong>&gt;Rs 100 cr AUM</strong> (non-institutional), <strong>&gt;Rs 1 cr</strong> commission p.a. industry-wide, <strong>&gt;Rs 50 lakh</strong> from <strong>one AMC</strong>.</li>\n<li>Yearly disclosure: distributor-wise gross and net inflows, average AUM, <strong>AUM/gross inflows</strong>, and whether a <strong>sponsor associate</strong>.</li>\n<li>Portfolio turnover <strong>&gt;2× industry average</strong> triggers <strong>extra due diligence</strong>.</li>\n<li>Due diligence checks: business model, penalties and suits, associates, and <strong>suitability processes separated from sales</strong>.</li>\n<li>MFD's advice is <strong>incidental</strong> to distribution, so no RIA registration needed; can't call himself an <strong>adviser or planner</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Only two</strong> transaction types: <strong>advisory</strong> (appropriateness) and <strong>execution only</strong>; <strong>no third category</strong>.</li>\n<li>Execution only when unsuitable: <strong>written warning</strong> acknowledged by the investor + <strong>written \"execution only\" confirmation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Mis-selling under <strong>PFUTP 2003</strong>: false statements, hiding facts, hiding risks, <strong>no suitability check</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Distributor nomination</strong>: commission goes to the nominee (or legal heir) until the investor changes the ARN or <strong>AUM becomes nil</strong>.</li>\n<li>Nominee <strong>need not hold an ARN</strong> to receive commission and receives it <strong>as trustee for legal heirs</strong>.</li>\n<li>ARN must be <strong>valid and not suspended on the date of death</strong>; <strong>no new business or SIPs</strong> under the deceased's ARN.</li>\n<li><strong>AUM transfer</strong> to a nominee needs a <strong>valid ARN + KYD</strong>; the nominee must <strong>inform all clients</strong> by letter or email, and investors get <strong>15 days</strong> to object to the AMC.</li>\n<li>Investor changes distributor: <strong>no commission to either</strong> the old or new distributor, <strong>except</strong> when the old distributor <strong>voluntarily ceased business</strong>.</li>\n<li>Investor-initiated ARN change: the AMC <strong>may</strong> pay trail to the new distributor after a <strong>12-month cooling-off</strong> period.</li>\n<li>Distributor-initiated code change allowed <strong>only</strong> for: change of <strong>name/legal status</strong>, <strong>merger/acquisition within group</strong>, <strong>family AUM consolidation</strong>, <strong>transfer of business</strong>; <strong>entire AUM</strong>, to a <strong>valid KYD ARN</strong>, <strong>old ARN surrendered</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li><strong>Multinational banks</strong> entered MF distribution first; <strong>PSU banks</strong> give non-urban reach.</li>\n<li>On exchanges, <strong>DPs can only process redemptions</strong>; exchanges are order-routing only, <strong>don't replace RTAs</strong>, and the <strong>AMC</strong> (not the exchange) is responsible for settlement.</li>\n<li>KYD biometric = <strong>right index finger</strong> at a CAMS PoS; the new cadre (V-B) is <strong>exempt</strong> from KYD biometrics.</li>\n<li>Employees get an <strong>EUIN</strong> under the firm's ARN, not a separate ARN; ARN minimum age is <strong>18</strong>.</li>\n<li>Due diligence triggers on <strong>any one</strong> of: &gt;20 locations, &gt;Rs 100 cr AUM, &gt;Rs 1 cr commission industry-wide, &gt;Rs 50 lakh from <strong>one AMC</strong>.</li>\n<li>Investor changes distributor: <strong>no commission to either</strong>, except when the old distributor voluntarily ceased business; investor-initiated ARN change → trail to new after a <strong>12-month</strong> cooling-off.</li>\n<li>AUM transfer to a nominee: investors get <strong>15 days</strong>, not 30, to object; the nominee needs <strong>valid ARN + KYD</strong> for an AUM transfer but <strong>no ARN</strong> just to receive commission.</li>\n<li>Additional commission is <strong>on top of</strong> trail, paid from the <strong>2 bps</strong> investor-education money, with <strong>no dual incentive</strong> for the same investor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-b (False; institutions too), 2-b (NISM), 3-a (True).</p>"},{"id":"ch7","n":7,"title":"NAV, TER and Pricing of Units","marks":8,"html":"<h3>Valuation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Valuation norms: <strong>Seventh Schedule</strong> (MF Regulations 2026); goal = <strong>fair treatment</strong> of entering, exiting and staying investors.</li>\n<li><strong>10 fair valuation principles</strong>; if they <strong>conflict with the Valuation Guidelines</strong>, the <strong>Principles prevail</strong>.</li>\n<li>Principle 1: value = <strong>realisable value</strong>, in <strong>good faith</strong>.</li>\n<li>Valuation policy approved by the <strong>AMC Board</strong>; a <strong>new security type</strong> needs its valuation method set <strong>first</strong>.</li>\n<li>Policy reviewed by an <strong>independent auditor at least once a year</strong>.</li>\n<li>Policy disclosed in the <strong>SAI</strong> and on the website.</li>\n<li>The <strong>AMC</strong> is responsible for <strong>true and fair</strong> valuation; it may <strong>deviate</strong> from policy (report to both <strong>Boards</strong>, disclose to investors).</li>\n<li>Valuation rationale kept for <strong>audit trail</strong> (<strong>Seventh Schedule</strong>).</li>\n<li>Debt valuation uses trade prices from <strong>all public platforms</strong>.</li>\n<li>Traded equity: <strong>last closing price</strong> on the <strong>principal exchange</strong> (reasons in <strong>writing</strong>).</li>\n<li>Not traded that day: use <strong>another exchange</strong>, or the <strong>earliest previous day within 30 days</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>No trade for 30 days = non-traded</strong>: valued <strong>in good faith</strong> by AMC Board methods; reviewed by trustees; auditors report them <strong>\"fair and reasonable\"</strong>.</li>\n<li>Non-traded equity: <strong>capitalisation of earnings</strong> (P/E of comparables) ± book value, <strong>less an illiquidity discount</strong>.</li>\n<li>Convertibles: value the debt and equity parts <strong>separately</strong>.</li>\n<li>Warrants: <strong>share value − exercise price − discount</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gold ETF</strong>: <strong>LBMA AM fixing</strong>, USD per troy ounce, <strong>995.0</strong> fineness. <strong>Silver ETF</strong>: LBMA AM fixing, <strong>999.0</strong> fineness.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>NAV</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>NAV = <strong>net assets ÷ units outstanding</strong> = <strong>(total assets − liabilities other than to unitholders) ÷ units</strong>.</li>\n<li>Workbook example: <strong>Rs 217 cr ÷ 20 cr = Rs 10.85</strong>.</li>\n<li>Net assets include <strong>unrealised gains</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Accrual principle</strong>: income and expenses count when <strong>due</strong>, not when paid.</li>\n<li>Short-term borrowing for liquidity <strong>≤ 6 months</strong>.</li>\n<li>NAV rises with more <strong>income and gains</strong> and falls with more <strong>expenses</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mark to market</strong> stops dilution: issuing units <strong>below NAV lowers</strong> NAV for existing investors; redeeming <strong>below NAV raises</strong> it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Expenses and TER</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>All scheme expenses are <strong>identified and paid from the scheme</strong>, within the <strong>base expense limits</strong>, brokerage limits, transaction cost and statutory levy.</li>\n<li>Recurring expenses (<strong>allowed</strong>, (a)–(o)): marketing and <strong>distribution</strong> fees/commission, brokerage, RTA, <strong>trustee fees</strong>, audit, custodian, investor communication, <strong>fund transfer</strong>, account statements and <strong>IDCW/redemption</strong> cheques, <strong>insurance</strong>, <strong>winding up</strong>, <strong>statutory ads</strong>, <strong>storage of goods</strong> from commodity derivatives / <strong>gold and silver</strong>, <strong>listing fee</strong>, and others SEBI approves.</li>\n<li>Spending <strong>above the base limits</strong> is borne by the <strong>AMC, trustees or sponsors</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>AMC pays</strong> distribution commission in the manner SEBI specifies.</li>\n<li><strong>Equity-oriented</strong> for TER = <strong>≥65%</strong> in equity (excluding index funds and ETFs).</li>\n<li><strong>Index fund / ETF</strong>: TER <strong>≤0.90%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>FoF</strong>: into liquid/index/ETF <strong>0.90%</strong>; <strong>≥65%</strong> into equity schemes <strong>2.10%</strong>; others <strong>1.85%</strong>; FoF's own layer <strong>≤2×</strong> the underlying's weighted TER.</li>\n<li>Open-ended TER slabs (equity / other):</li>\n<li>first <strong>Rs 500 cr</strong>: <strong>2.10 / 1.85</strong></li>\n<li>next <strong>250</strong>: <strong>1.90 / 1.65</strong></li>\n<li>next <strong>1,250</strong>: <strong>1.60 / 1.40</strong></li>\n<li>next <strong>3,000</strong>: <strong>1.50 / 1.25</strong></li>\n<li>next <strong>5,000</strong>: <strong>1.40 / 1.15</strong></li>\n<li>next <strong>40,000</strong>: <strong>−0.05% per Rs 5,000 cr</strong></li>\n<li>balance: <strong>0.95 / 0.70</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Close-ended / interval</strong>: equity <strong>1.00%</strong>, others <strong>0.80%</strong>.</li>\n<li>On top of the limits: brokerage <strong>≤0.06%</strong> (cash) and <strong>≤0.02%</strong> (derivatives) of trade value.</li>\n<li>Total charged to investors = <strong>base limit + brokerage + transaction cost + statutory levies</strong>; nothing else except <strong>exit load</strong>.</li>\n<li>The old extra <strong>0.05%</strong> for schemes with exit load is <strong>gone</strong>.</li>\n<li>TER disclosed <strong>daily</strong> on the AMC and AMFI sites.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>AT1 and AT2 bonds</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Bank <strong>perpetual AT1/AT2 bonds</strong>: MF <strong>≤10%</strong> of one issuer's bonds; scheme <strong>≤10%</strong> of assets; <strong>≤5%</strong> in one issuer.</li>\n<li>Deemed maturity of <strong>AT1</strong> from <strong>1 Apr 2023 = 100 years from issue</strong>; <strong>AT2 = contractual maturity</strong>.</li>\n<li>AT1 valued on <strong>yield to call</strong>; <strong>close-ended debt</strong> schemes <strong>can't</strong> buy perpetuals.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Dividends (IDCW) and loads</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Paid <strong>only from distributable surplus</strong>: <strong>valuation gains ignored</strong>, <strong>valuation losses deducted</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trustees</strong> decide the amount and <strong>record date</strong>; monthly or more frequent payouts can be <strong>delegated to AMC officials</strong> (ratified at the <strong>next board meeting</strong>).</li>\n<li>Public notice <strong>within 1 day</strong> of the decision; record date <strong>5 calendar days</strong> from notice; notice says <strong>NAV will fall</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>No notice</strong> needed for <strong>daily-to-monthly</strong> payouts in liquid and debt schemes.</li>\n<li><strong>Late payment</strong> = interest <strong>from the record date</strong>.</li>\n<li>Payout may include <strong>capital</strong> (from the <strong>equalisation reserve</strong>); the split must be disclosed.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry load banned</strong>, so sale price = <strong>NAV</strong> (+ stamp duty).</li>\n<li><strong>Exit load</strong>: repurchase price = <strong>NAV × (1 − load)</strong>; e.g. Rs 11 with 1% = <strong>Rs 10.89</strong>.</li>\n<li>Exit load: <strong>no distinction by amount</strong>; changes apply <strong>only prospectively</strong>; <strong>none on bonus or IDCW-reinvestment units</strong>; credited <strong>back to the scheme</strong>.</li>\n<li>Rs 25,000 at NAV Rs 43.21 = <strong>578.570 units</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Accounting and segregated portfolio</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Scheme accounts <strong>separate</strong> from AMC accounts, with <strong>different auditors</strong>.</li>\n<li>NAV decimals: <strong>4</strong> for index, liquid and debt funds; <strong>≥2</strong> for equity and balanced funds.</li>\n<li><strong>Fractional units</strong> allowed; exchanges may trade <strong>whole units</strong> only.</li>\n<li><strong>Segregated portfolio</strong>: <strong>no management fee</strong>; other TER only <strong>pro-rata on recovery</strong>; NAV declared <strong>daily</strong>.</li>\n<li>Unrated debt can be segregated <strong>only on actual default</strong> (issuer with no rated debt).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li>If the fair valuation principles conflict with the Valuation Guidelines, the <strong>principles</strong> prevail, not the guidelines.</li>\n<li>The valuation policy is approved by the <strong>AMC Board</strong>, not the trustees, and reviewed by an independent auditor at least <strong>once a year</strong>.</li>\n<li>No trade for <strong>30 days</strong> = non-traded; non-traded equity is valued with an illiquidity <strong>discount</strong>, not a premium.</li>\n<li>Gold ETF: LBMA <strong>AM</strong> fixing at <strong>995.0</strong> fineness (USD per troy ounce); Silver ETF: LBMA AM fixing at <strong>999.0</strong>.</li>\n<li>NAV deducts liabilities <strong>other than to unitholders</strong>; net assets <strong>include</strong> unrealised gains.</li>\n<li>Issuing units below NAV <strong>lowers</strong> NAV for existing investors; redeeming below NAV <strong>raises</strong> it.</li>\n<li>Distributable surplus: valuation <strong>gains ignored</strong>, valuation <strong>losses deducted</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trustees</strong> decide the IDCW amount and record date, not the AMC; notice within <strong>1 day</strong>, record date <strong>5 calendar days</strong> from notice.</li>\n<li>Close-ended / interval TER: equity <strong>1.00%</strong>, others <strong>0.80%</strong>; index fund / ETF <strong>0.90%</strong>.</li>\n<li>AT1 deemed maturity = <strong>100 years</strong> from issue; AT2 = <strong>contractual</strong> maturity; AT1 valued on yield to <strong>call</strong>, not maturity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-a (SAI), 2-b (Rs 10.20), 3-a (0.90%; the workbook misprints it as \"90 percent\"), 4-c (distributable surplus).</p>"},{"id":"ch8","n":8,"title":"Taxation","marks":4,"html":"<ul>\n<li>MF scheme income is <strong>tax exempt</strong> under <strong>Sec 10(23D)</strong>, because MFs are <strong>trusts</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth</strong> option = <strong>capital gains only</strong>; <strong>IDCW</strong> option = dividend <strong>+</strong> capital gains.</li>\n<li>Tax depends on: <strong>type of income</strong>, <strong>holding period</strong>, <strong>type of scheme</strong>, <strong>type of investor</strong>, <strong>purchase date</strong> (<strong>1 Apr 2023</strong>) and <strong>redemption date</strong> (<strong>23 Jul 2024</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Equity-oriented</strong> for tax = <strong>&gt;65%</strong> in <strong>listed Indian equity</strong>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>FoF investing in equity MFs</strong> is <strong>non-equity</strong> for tax.</li>\n<li>Joint holding: income taxed in the <strong>first holder's</strong> hands.</li>\n<li>Holding periods: only <strong>12 or 24 months</strong>; <strong>12 months</strong> for <strong>listed securities and equity funds</strong>, <strong>24 months</strong> for others.</li>\n<li><strong>Grandfathering</strong>: gains up to <strong>31 Jan 2018</strong> exempt; cost = <strong>higher of actual cost or 31 Jan 2018 NAV</strong>.</li>\n<li>Grandfathering example: cost Rs 1 lakh, 31 Jan 2018 value Rs 2 lakh; sold at <strong>Rs 1.8 lakh = zero gain</strong>; at <strong>Rs 2.2 lakh = Rs 20,000</strong> gain.</li>\n<li>Equity LTCG exempt up to <strong>Rs 1.25 lakh</strong> a year: Rs 90,000 gain = <strong>no tax</strong>; Rs 1.35 lakh = tax on <strong>Rs 10,000</strong>.</li>\n<li>Workbook tax table (STCG / LTCG / LTCG holding period):</li>\n<li><strong>Equity-oriented</strong> (incl. hybrids <strong>&gt;65% equity</strong>): <strong>20% / 12.5%</strong>, after <strong>12 months</strong>, <strong>Rs 1.25 lakh</strong> exempt per FY</li>\n<li><strong>Debt-oriented</strong> (incl. hybrids <strong>&gt;65% debt</strong>): <strong>marginal slab rate</strong> (MSR)</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid with 35–65% domestic equity</strong>: <strong>MSR / 12.5%</strong>, after <strong>24 months</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Other MFs</strong> (FoFs, international funds/FoFs): <strong>MSR / 12.5%</strong>, after <strong>24 months</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Equity ETFs</strong> and <strong>Gold ETFs</strong>: <strong>MSR / 12.5%</strong>, after <strong>12 months</strong> (as printed)</li>\n<li><strong>Debt ETFs</strong>: <strong>MSR</strong></li>\n<li><strong>DDT abolished</strong> by Budget <strong>Feb 2020</strong>; since <strong>April 2020</strong> IDCW is taxed at the investor's <strong>slab rate</strong>; tax-exempt investors pay <strong>nil</strong>.</li>\n<li>IDCW <strong>reduces NAV by more</strong> than the investor's post-tax receipt.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth option is more tax-efficient</strong> because of <strong>tax deferral</strong>.</li>\n<li>From <strong>1 Apr 2021</strong>, IDCW is split into <strong>income distribution</strong> and <strong>capital distribution</strong> for tax.</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp duty</strong> (from <strong>1 Jul 2020</strong>): <strong>0.005%</strong> on <strong>issue</strong> (purchase, SIP, STP, switch-in, IDCW reinvestment); <strong>0.015%</strong> on <strong>transfer</strong>.</li>\n<li>Units are allotted on the amount <strong>after stamp duty</strong>.</li>\n<li>Capital loss <strong>can't be set off against salary</strong> or other income heads.</li>\n<li><strong>STCL</strong> sets off against <strong>STCG or LTCG</strong>; <strong>LTCL</strong> only against <strong>LTCG</strong>.</li>\n<li>Unabsorbed capital loss can be <strong>carried forward up to 8 years</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bonus stripping</strong>: loss <strong>disallowed</strong> if bought <strong>≤3 months before</strong> the record date and sold <strong>≤9 months after</strong>; the loss becomes the <strong>cost of the bonus units</strong>. Even outside this window, the practice is <strong>discouraged</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>STT</strong> applies only to <strong>equity-oriented funds</strong>, on <strong>sale or redemption</strong>; <strong>none on purchase</strong>; <strong>none on debt funds</strong>.</li>\n<li>STT rates: redemption to the MF <strong>0.001%</strong>; delivery sale on exchange <strong>0.001%</strong>; non-delivery <strong>0.025%</strong>; all paid by the <strong>seller</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>ELSS (Tax Saver)</strong>: 80C up to <strong>Rs 1.5 lakh</strong> (individual/<strong>HUF</strong>), <strong>Old Tax Regime only</strong>, <strong>3-year lock-in</strong>.</li>\n<li>ELSS lock-in runs from <strong>each SIP instalment</strong> and <strong>each IDCW reinvestment</strong>; the tax benefit goes to the <strong>first holder</strong>.</li>\n<li>Under the <strong>new tax regime</strong>, ELSS is just an <strong>equity fund with a lock-in</strong>.</li>\n<li>Some <strong>old retirement funds</strong> also give <strong>80C</strong>, with a <strong>5-year lock-in</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>No TDS</strong> on redemption for <strong>residents</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>TDS on IDCW</strong> for residents: <strong>10%</strong> if dividends exceed <strong>Rs 10,000</strong> a year.</li>\n<li>NRIs: TDS at the <strong>lower of the Income Tax Act rate or the DTAA rate</strong> (with documents).</li>\n<li><strong>GST on management fee</strong> is charged <strong>over and above</strong> TER; GST on <strong>other fees</strong> and on <strong>brokerage/transaction cost</strong> sits <strong>within</strong> TER.</li>\n<li>From <strong>1 April 2026</strong>: cost = <strong>base expense ratio + brokerage (within limits) + statutory levies (e.g. GST)</strong>. BER = advisory fees + other expenses + brokerage commission, <strong>excluding statutory levies</strong> and transaction cost; nothing else may be charged except <strong>exit load</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>GST on exit load</strong> is deducted <strong>from the load</strong> before crediting the scheme.</li>\n<li><strong>GST on distributor commission</strong> can't be charged to the scheme.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li>A FoF investing in equity MFs is <strong>non-equity</strong> for tax, not equity-oriented; equity-oriented = <strong>more than 65%</strong> in <strong>listed Indian</strong> equity.</li>\n<li>Hybrids with 35-65% domestic equity and other MFs (FoFs, international) turn long-term after <strong>24 months</strong>, not 12; equity funds after <strong>12 months</strong>.</li>\n<li>Grandfathering cut-off is <strong>31 Jan 2018</strong>; cost = <strong>higher</strong> of actual cost or 31 Jan 2018 NAV, not lower.</li>\n<li>Stamp duty: <strong>0.005%</strong> on issue, <strong>0.015%</strong> on transfer, from <strong>1 Jul 2020</strong>; units are allotted on the amount <strong>after</strong> stamp duty.</li>\n<li>STCL sets off against <strong>STCG or LTCG</strong>; LTCL <strong>only against LTCG</strong>; neither against salary; carry forward <strong>8 years</strong>.</li>\n<li>STT applies only on <strong>sale/redemption</strong> of <strong>equity-oriented</strong> funds and is paid by the <strong>seller</strong>; none on purchase, none on debt funds.</li>\n<li>NRI TDS is at the <strong>lower</strong> of the IT Act rate or DTAA rate, not the higher; residents have <strong>no TDS</strong> on redemption.</li>\n<li>GST on <strong>management fee</strong> is <strong>over and above</strong> TER; GST on other fees and brokerage is <strong>within</strong> TER; GST on distributor commission can't be charged to the scheme.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-b (MF income exempt), 2-a (multi-cap attracts STT).</p>"},{"id":"ch9","n":9,"title":"Investor Services","marks":15,"html":"<h3>NFO and plans</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Scheme idea comes from the <strong>CIO</strong> (objective) and <strong>CMO</strong> (market interest).</li>\n<li><strong>One scheme per category</strong>; exceptions: <strong>sector/thematic, index, FoF</strong>.</li>\n<li>SID approved by the <strong>trustees + AMC Board</strong>, filed with SEBI, SEBI's observations incorporated.</li>\n<li>Open-ended NFO dates: <strong>open, close, re-opening</strong>; close-ended has <strong>no re-opening date</strong>.</li>\n<li>NFO open <strong>min 3 working days</strong>, <strong>max 15 days</strong> (<strong>ELSS exempt</strong> from the maximum).</li>\n<li><strong>Allot or refund within 5 business days</strong> of NFO close; late refund = <strong>15% p.a.</strong> interest, <strong>paid by the AMC</strong>.</li>\n<li>Open-ended scheme <strong>re-opens within 5 business days of allotment</strong>.</li>\n<li>Realistic deployment timelines in the SID (<strong>Reg 35(5)</strong>).</li>\n<li>NFO price = <strong>Rs 10</strong> (no entry load): units = amount ÷ 10.</li>\n<li>No entry load, so <strong>sale price = NAV</strong>; redemption price = NAV <strong>less any exit load</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Demat credit within 2 working days</strong>; SoA within <strong>5 business days</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>ASBA</strong>: money is only <strong>blocked</strong>, debited <strong>on allotment</strong>, keeps earning interest, <strong>no refund wait</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trustees</strong> can reject any application <strong>without giving a reason</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Direct plan</strong>: <strong>lower TER</strong>, <strong>no commission</strong>, <strong>separate NAV</strong>; anything through a distributor = <strong>Regular</strong>.</li>\n<li>Only <strong>two plans</strong> (Direct and Regular) can differ by expense.</li>\n<li>Direct application: write <strong>\"Direct\"</strong> in the <strong>ARN box</strong>.</li>\n<li>IDCW <strong>payout</strong>: cash to bank, units <strong>unchanged</strong>, NAV falls (<strong>ex-dividend</strong> NAV); <strong>cum-dividend</strong> NAV = between announcement and payout.</li>\n<li>IDCW <strong>reinvestment</strong>: units added at <strong>ex-dividend NAV</strong>; e.g. Rs 2 × 100 units at NAV 20 = <strong>10 units</strong>; the Rs 200 is still <strong>taxed at slab</strong> in the year received.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth</strong>: no dividend; NAV captures <strong>all gains</strong>.</li>\n<li>Workbook illustration: NAV 10 → 12, dividend <strong>10% of face value = Re 1</strong>; C reinvests at <strong>Rs 11</strong> = <strong>9.09 units</strong>; A = Rs 1,200, B = Rs 1,100 + Rs 100, C = Rs 1,200.</li>\n<li>Ongoing purchase: Rs 12,000 at Rs 12 = <strong>1,000 units</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bonus 1:3</strong> = 1 free unit per 3 held; NAV falls <strong>proportionately</strong>; <strong>value unchanged</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SIF</strong>: <strong>Rs 10 lakh</strong> minimum at <strong>PAN level</strong>, across SIF strategies only.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Account statements</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SoA</strong> issued each month with a transaction.</li>\n<li>Investors with <strong>no transaction in 6 months</strong>: statement <strong>annually</strong> (or email).</li>\n<li><strong>CAS monthly</strong> if any transaction that month; <strong>half-yearly CAS</strong> if none; investors matched by <strong>PAN</strong>.</li>\n<li>\"Transaction\" includes purchase, redemption, switch, IDCW payout and reinvestment, SIP, SWP, STP, <strong>bonus</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Eligible investors and the form</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Individuals: adults, up to <strong>3 joint holders</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Minors</strong> invest via a guardian (<strong>natural parent or court-appointed</strong>); <strong>no joint holders</strong>.</li>\n<li>Minor's payment only from the <strong>minor's, parent's/guardian's, or minor-guardian joint account</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>HUF</strong> invests via the <strong>Karta</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>NRI/PIO/OCI</strong>: <strong>repatriable</strong> or <strong>non-repatriable</strong> basis.</li>\n<li><strong>QFI</strong> must be from a <strong>FATF</strong> member and an <strong>IOSCO MMOU</strong> signatory.</li>\n<li><strong>FPIs</strong>: <strong>direct route</strong> (<strong>demat</strong>) or <strong>indirect route</strong> (<strong>UCR</strong>, Unit Confirmation Receipt).</li>\n<li>Check <strong>\"Who can invest\"</strong> in the SID (e.g. some gilt plans only for PFs and trusts).</li>\n<li>The <strong>first holder</strong> gets all benefits (dividends, redemptions, tax).</li>\n<li><strong>PoA holder</strong> can do everything <strong>except nominate</strong>; the grantor can still operate.</li>\n<li>Joint holders <strong>can't be added or removed</strong>, except a deletion on <strong>death</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>KYC address overrides</strong> the address on the application form.</li>\n<li>Form also collects <strong>occupation, income/net worth, PEP status</strong>; <strong>FATCA/CRS</strong> for foreign links.</li>\n<li>Bank details of the <strong>first holder are mandatory</strong>.</li>\n<li>Proof for a different payout account: cancelled cheque, passbook/statement <strong>≤3 months old</strong>, or bank letter.</li>\n<li>Demat applicants: details must <strong>match the DP</strong>; <strong>DP's KYC is accepted</strong>; payout goes to the <strong>demat-linked bank account</strong>.</li>\n<li>Nomination: <strong>opt in or opt out</strong> by signed declaration; <strong>optional for joint folios</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Transactions and payments</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Transaction slip</strong> for additional purchases (folio details <strong>override</strong> the form).</li>\n<li>Redemption by <strong>amount or units</strong>; <strong>FIFO</strong>.</li>\n<li>If the balance falls below the minimum, the <strong>whole folio</strong> may be redeemed.</li>\n<li><strong>Demat redemption goes through the DP</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pool accounts of brokers discontinued</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Two-factor authentication</strong> mandatory for subscriptions and redemptions.</li>\n<li>SIF may have <strong>different subscription and redemption frequencies</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>NACH</strong> (by <strong>NPCI</strong>) replaced <strong>ECS</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>UPI</strong> uses a <strong>VPA</strong>; <strong>AEPS</strong> uses <strong>Aadhaar + fingerprint</strong>; <strong>USSD *99#</strong> works without internet.</li>\n<li><strong>Credit cards not allowed</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>E-wallet</strong> + <strong>cash</strong>: <strong>Rs 50,000</strong> per investor <strong>per MF per FY</strong> (umbrella limit).</li>\n<li>E-wallet money only if loaded via <strong>cash, debit card or net banking</strong>; <strong>no third-party</strong> payments.</li>\n<li><strong>Cash</strong> (subject to conditions) only for <strong>resident individuals, sole proprietors, minors</strong> (KYC-compliant); payouts <strong>only via bank</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>OTM</strong> (One-Time Mandate) is at <strong>folio level</strong>, runs on NACH, frequency <strong>\"as and when presented\"</strong>, takes <strong>3–4 weeks</strong> to register.</li>\n<li>Cheques: <strong>not post-dated</strong> (except SIP), <strong>not stale</strong> (<strong>&gt;3 months</strong>), <strong>local or at-par</strong>, first holder must be an account holder.</li>\n<li>NRI repatriable investment only from <strong>NRE/FCNR</strong> (with <strong>FIRC</strong>); non-repatriable also from <strong>NRO</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Third-party payment exceptions</strong> (only three): <strong>parent or related person</strong> for a <strong>minor</strong> up to <strong>Rs 50,000</strong> per purchase or SIP instalment; <strong>employer</strong> payroll; <strong>custodian for FPI</strong>.</li>\n<li>Third-party payer must be <strong>KYC-compliant with PAN</strong> + declaration; <strong>DD against cash ≥ Rs 50,000 rejected</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Not accepted</strong>: stock-invests, postal/money orders, outstation cheques, post-dated cheques (except SIP), cash beyond the exception.</li>\n<li>Multiple bank accounts: <strong>5</strong> for individuals, <strong>10</strong> for non-individuals; first holder must be on each; one is <strong>default</strong>.</li>\n<li>A payout to an <strong>unregistered</strong> account goes to the <strong>default</strong> account.</li>\n<li>NRO-paid investment gets <strong>NRO</strong> payouts only; NRE-paid can go to <strong>NRO or NRE</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Instant Access Facility</strong>: <strong>liquid and overnight</strong> schemes; <strong>lower of Rs 50,000 or 90%</strong> of value, <strong>per day per scheme per investor</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Cut-off times</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Equity and debt purchases (not liquid/overnight)</strong>: <strong>3:00 pm</strong>; NAV of the day <strong>funds are available</strong> for use, <strong>any amount</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Liquid and overnight purchases</strong>: <strong>1:30 pm</strong>; funds in before cut-off = NAV of the <strong>day before</strong> receipt; after cut-off = NAV of the <strong>day before the next business day</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Equity/debt redemption</strong>: <strong>3 pm</strong>; <strong>same day</strong> NAV before, <strong>next business day</strong> after.</li>\n<li><strong>Liquid redemption</strong>: <strong>3 pm</strong>; NAV of the <strong>day before the next business day</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Overnight redemption</strong>: <strong>3 pm</strong>, <strong>online 7 pm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cut-offs <strong>don't apply</strong> to <strong>NFOs and international schemes</strong>.</li>\n<li>Liquid funds calculate NAV for <strong>every calendar day</strong>.</li>\n<li>Time stamping is at the <strong>OPoA</strong> (not when the distributor receives the form); machines are <strong>tamper-proof</strong>; opening one is <strong>reported to trustees</strong>.</li>\n<li>Serial numbers <strong>don't restart daily</strong>; non-financial requests need only the <strong>date</strong>.</li>\n<li>Online time = <strong>web server time</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>KYC</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everyone</strong> needs KYC: joint holders, NRIs, <strong>PoA holder and issuer</strong>, <strong>guardian</strong> (not the minor).</li>\n<li><strong>PAN exempt</strong>: government and court officials, <strong>Sikkim</strong> residents, UN bodies, and <strong>Micro-SIP + lump sum ≤ Rs 50,000</strong> per year per MF (quote <strong>PEKRN</strong>).</li>\n<li>Micro-SIP ID proofs include a <strong>photo debit card</strong> (<strong>not a credit card</strong>).</li>\n<li>Address proof in the <strong>spouse's name</strong> is accepted.</li>\n<li><strong>KRA</strong>: one KYC across the capital market; <strong>IPV</strong> mandatory (a bank's IPV is accepted).</li>\n<li><strong>CERSAI</strong> = <strong>Central KYC Records Registry</strong> (notified <strong>26 Nov 2015</strong>); upload for individual accounts from <strong>1 Aug 2016</strong>; issues a <strong>KYC Identifier</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Aadhaar e-KYC</strong> via <strong>KUA / sub-KUA</strong>.</li>\n<li>Online KYC via <strong>eSign, DigiLocker, OTP, penny drop</strong>.</li>\n<li>NRI KYC: <strong>PAN</strong>, passport/PIO/OCI, <strong>overseas address</strong>.</li>\n<li>Non-individuals: eligibility, <strong>board resolution</strong>, <strong>authorised signatories</strong>, <strong>UBO</strong>.</li>\n<li>UBO thresholds: <strong>company &gt;25%</strong>; <strong>partnership/body of individuals &gt;15%</strong>; <strong>trust ≥15%</strong> beneficiary + settlor + trustees; <strong>not for listed companies</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>FATCA/CRS</strong> status change reported <strong>within 30 days</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Systematic transactions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SIP</strong> = <strong>rupee cost averaging</strong>: average cost <strong>below</strong> the average NAV; can start <strong>during an NFO</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SWP</strong> with 1% load at NAV 10: <strong>101.0101 units</strong> for Rs 1,000; suits regular expenses, e.g. <strong>in retirement</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>STP</strong> = SWP from the <strong>source</strong> + SIP into the <strong>target</strong>; scheme minimums <strong>don't apply</strong>; loads and tax <strong>do</strong>.</li>\n<li>STP example: 0.25% load on Rs 1 lakh = <strong>Rs 99,750</strong> transferred.</li>\n<li><strong>DTP</strong> (dividend transfer plan): needs the <strong>IDCW option</strong>; <strong>overrides</strong> payout/reinvest; invested the <strong>next business day after the record date</strong>; ends when source units = <strong>nil</strong>.</li>\n<li>Minor's SIP registered <strong>only till majority</strong>.</li>\n<li>SIP registration takes <strong>15–30 days</strong>: submit 5 Sept for a 30-day lead with SIP date 15th = first debit <strong>15 Oct</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SIP top-up</strong>: +Rs 500 a year on Rs 2,500 = <strong>3,000, then 3,500</strong>; +10% = <strong>2,750, then 3,025</strong>.</li>\n<li>An SIP may be <strong>cancelled</strong> for <strong>insufficient funds</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Triggers</strong> are <strong>one-time</strong>: stop-loss, appreciation, date or market-level.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Non-financial transactions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Up to 10 nominees</strong> per folio; <strong>no limit</strong> on how often a nomination can be made, changed or cancelled.</li>\n<li>Nomination covers the <strong>whole folio</strong>; % must total <strong>100</strong>, else <strong>equal</strong>.</li>\n<li>Joint holder dies: units pass to the <strong>surviving holder(s)</strong> by <strong>name deletion</strong>, as <strong>owners</strong>, not trustees.</li>\n<li>Nomination only by <strong>individuals</strong>; <strong>not</strong> for a <strong>minor's folio</strong> or by a <strong>PoA holder</strong>; <strong>all joint holders sign</strong>.</li>\n<li>Nominee can be an <strong>individual (even a minor or NRI)</strong>, <strong>government or local authority</strong>, or a <strong>religious/charitable trust</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Not</strong> a nominee: other trusts, society, company, firm, <strong>Karta</strong>, PoA holder.</li>\n<li>A new nomination <strong>supersedes</strong> the old one.</li>\n<li>Demat units follow the <strong>demat account's nomination</strong>.</li>\n<li>Nominee holds units <strong>in trust for legal heirs</strong>.</li>\n<li>An investor can let <strong>one adult nominee</strong> operate the folio if the investor is <strong>physically incapacitated</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pledge</strong>: all holders sign; units must be <strong>out of lock-in</strong>; can't sell until the <strong>pledgee's NOC</strong>; the lender can redeem <strong>below the minimum</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>ISIN</strong> for <strong>each option</strong>; demat to physical = <strong>rematerialisation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Name, address, PAN, contact changes go via the <strong>KRA</strong>; bank, mode of holding and nomination changes go to <strong>each MF</strong>.</li>\n<li>Change of guardian needs the <strong>new guardian's KYC + NOC</strong> from the old guardian.</li>\n<li><strong>Transmission</strong>: first holder dies = <strong>second holder</strong> becomes first; single holder with nominee = <strong>nominee</strong>; joint holder <strong>ranks above</strong> nominee; no nominee = <strong>legal heirs</strong>.</li>\n<li>Transmission needs <strong>KYC</strong>, <strong>death certificate</strong>, <strong>indemnity</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Minor turns major</strong>: folio <strong>frozen</strong> for the guardian; SIP/SWP/STP <strong>stop</strong>; submit the <strong>MAM form</strong> with PAN, KYC, bank proof, <strong>banker-attested signature</strong>, nomination, new SIP mandate.</li>\n<li>IDCW payout or reinvestment <strong>continues</strong> after majority.</li>\n<li>Minor's <strong>demat</strong> is single-name, so a <strong>new demat account</strong> is needed at majority.</li>\n<li><strong>NRI returns to India</strong>: NRE/NRO/FCNR can't be operated; open a <strong>resident account</strong> (<strong>RFC</strong> for foreign currency) and a <strong>new resident demat</strong>; update the <strong>KRA</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Change of Karta</strong>: death certificate, bank certificate, <strong>indemnity from all co-parceners</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Turnaround times</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unit certificate within 5 working days</strong>; <strong>demat for close-ended within 2 working days</strong>.</li>\n<li>SoA or demat credit within <strong>5 working days</strong> of NFO close or application.</li>\n<li><strong>Portfolio statement</strong> within <strong>10 days</strong> of the half-year.</li>\n<li><strong>Annual report</strong> within <strong>4 months</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Unaudited half-yearly results</strong> within <strong>1 month</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Delay interest</strong> on redemption or dividend is borne by the <strong>AMC</strong>, never the scheme.</li>\n<li><strong>MITRA</strong> = a tracing platform for <strong>inactive and unclaimed folios</strong>, run by <strong>CAMS and KFintech</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>DigiLocker</strong> will hold MF statements and has its own <strong>nominee</strong> access.</li>\n<li><strong>Voluntary lock-in / debit freeze</strong> (SEBI, <strong>March 2026</strong>): investors can freeze demat and non-demat folios so <strong>no units can be debited</strong> until unlocked; first offered by RTAs on <strong>MF Central</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li>Late NFO refund interest at <strong>15% p.a.</strong> is paid by the <strong>AMC</strong>, not the scheme.</li>\n<li>NFO stays open <strong>min 3 working days, max 15 days</strong>; <strong>ELSS</strong> is exempt from the maximum, not the minimum.</li>\n<li>Only <strong>open-ended</strong> NFOs have a re-opening date (within <strong>5 business days of allotment</strong>); close-ended have none.</li>\n<li>IDCW reinvestment is at the <strong>ex-dividend</strong> NAV, not the cum-dividend NAV; the reinvested amount is still <strong>taxed at slab</strong>.</li>\n<li>Bonus <strong>1:3</strong> = <strong>1</strong> free unit for every <strong>3</strong> held, not 3 for 1; value is unchanged.</li>\n<li>Liquid/overnight purchase cut-off is <strong>1:30 pm</strong>; redemption cut-off is <strong>3 pm</strong> for all, with overnight online at <strong>7 pm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Instant Access Facility = the <strong>lower</strong> of Rs 50,000 or 90% of value, not the higher.</li>\n<li>UBO: company <strong>&gt;25%</strong>; partnership/body of individuals <strong>&gt;15%</strong>; trust <strong>≥15%</strong>; not for listed companies.</li>\n<li>Time stamp is at the <strong>OPoA</strong>, not when the distributor receives the form.</li>\n<li>Nominee holds units <strong>in trust for legal heirs</strong>; a surviving joint holder takes them as <strong>owner</strong>, and ranks <strong>above</strong> the nominee.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-a (ex-dividend), 2-b (bonus free), 3-c (foreign investors after KYC), 4-b (guardian), 5-d (5), 6-c (both), 7-d (any of the above), 8-a (SIF).</p>"},{"id":"ch10","n":10,"title":"Risk, Return and Performance of Funds","marks":7,"html":"<h3>Risk factors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The SID lists <strong>standard (general)</strong> and <strong>specific</strong> risk factors, plus <strong>mitigation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Standard statements: past performance <strong>no guarantee</strong>; scheme <strong>name says nothing about quality</strong>; sponsor not liable beyond its <strong>initial contribution</strong>.</li>\n<li>General risks: liquidity, interest rate, <strong>reinvestment</strong> (\"interest on interest\"), political, economic, <strong>foreign currency</strong> (for FPIs; the <strong>AMC doesn't manage</strong> it), settlement/counterparty, exchange-transaction.</li>\n<li><strong>Money market</strong> securities are liquid but have <strong>no well-developed secondary market</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Securities lending</strong>: borrower returns equivalent securities plus corporate benefits; risk = counterparty failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Short-selling</strong> risks: counterparty and <strong>illiquidity</strong> of the borrowed stock.</li>\n<li>Moving down from <strong>large to mid to small caps</strong> increases volatility and liquidity risk.</li>\n<li>Derivatives are <strong>leveraged</strong>. Risk types: <strong>counterparty</strong> (the exchange guarantees settlement), <strong>market liquidity</strong>, <strong>model</strong> (mispricing), <strong>basis</strong> (hedge vs underlying).</li>\n<li>Debt risks: reinvestment, <strong>rating migration</strong> (AAA → AA+ = price falls), term structure, credit.</li>\n<li>Floaters: <strong>spread risk</strong> and <strong>basis risk</strong>.</li>\n<li>Repo in corporate bonds: counterparty risk, mitigated by <strong>over-collateralisation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Segregated portfolio: may be <strong>illiquid</strong>, may realise <strong>nothing</strong>, listing <strong>doesn't ensure</strong> liquidity.</li>\n<li><strong>Securitisation</strong>: originator sells receivables to an <strong>SPV (trust)</strong>, which issues <strong>PTCs</strong>.</li>\n<li>Securitisation pool checks: <strong>lower LTV = safer</strong>; <strong>seasoning</strong> (more instalments already paid = better); <strong>DPD</strong> buckets.</li>\n<li><strong>Credit enhancement</strong> lets PTCs be rated <strong>above the originator</strong>; they are <strong>non-recourse</strong>.</li>\n<li>Securitisation risks: <strong>prepayment</strong>, originator bankruptcy (<strong>true sale</strong>), <strong>co-mingling</strong>.</li>\n<li>MFs mostly buy securitised paper rated <strong>AA and above</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Systematic</strong> = market = <strong>non-diversifiable</strong> (e.g. inflation). <strong>Unsystematic</strong> = company-specific = <strong>diversifiable</strong> (e.g. a strike).</li>\n<li>Investors are <strong>rewarded only for systematic risk</strong>.</li>\n<li>The fund manager can cut systematic risk only by <strong>holding cash</strong> (limited by SEBI).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Drivers of return</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Equity: <strong>security selection</strong> and <strong>market timing</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fundamental analysis</strong> suits the <strong>long term</strong>; <strong>technical analysis</strong> (price-volume \"<strong>chartists</strong>\") suits the <strong>short term and timing</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>EPS</strong> = PAT ÷ shares; <strong>P/E</strong> = price ÷ EPS; <strong>forward P/E</strong> uses projected EPS.</li>\n<li><strong>PEG = 1</strong> fair; <strong>&lt;1</strong> undervalued; <strong>&gt;1</strong> overvalued.</li>\n<li><strong>Book value/share</strong> = net worth ÷ shares; <strong>P/BV</strong> = price ÷ BV.</li>\n<li><strong>Dividend yield</strong> = DPS ÷ price; it <strong>falls in bull markets</strong>, <strong>rises in bear markets</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth</strong> stocks: <strong>high P/E and PEG, low dividend yield</strong>; fall more in corrections.</li>\n<li><strong>Value</strong> stocks: priced <strong>below intrinsic value</strong>; need a <strong>long horizon</strong>.</li>\n<li>High <strong>valuation</strong> ≠ high <strong>price</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>EIC</strong> framework: <strong>Economy, Industry, Company</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Top-down</strong>: economy first, avoids a bad <strong>sector</strong>. <strong>Bottom-up</strong>: stock first, sector allocation is the <strong>result</strong>.</li>\n<li>Debt returns are driven by <strong>interest rates and credit spreads</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>T-bills</strong>: issued by <strong>RBI</strong> for the GoI. <strong>CDs</strong>: banks <strong>7 days–1 year</strong>, FIs <strong>1–3 years</strong>. <strong>CP</strong>: companies, <strong>up to 1 year</strong>.</li>\n<li>Government issues <strong>bonds</strong>; private companies issue <strong>debentures</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Credit spread</strong> = corporate yield − <strong>gilt</strong> yield; rating agencies: <strong>CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings (Fitch)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>YTM</strong> = return if <strong>held to maturity</strong>; <strong>HPR</strong> = interest + capital gain or loss.</li>\n<li>Floater coupon = <strong>base + spread</strong>; it <strong>holds its value</strong> when rates move.</li>\n<li>Rates <strong>expected up</strong>: shorten duration or buy floaters. Rates <strong>expected down</strong>: buy <strong>long</strong> duration.</li>\n<li>A 9.5% AAA bond when market yield is 8.5% trades at a <strong>premium</strong>.</li>\n<li>Debt fund returns hinge on <strong>interest-rate calls</strong>; equity funds on <strong>sector and stock</strong> calls.</li>\n<li>Liquid, ultra-short and floater funds focus on <strong>accrual</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gold</strong> returns = <strong>global price + rupee</strong> (<strong>weaker rupee = higher</strong> return) + duties; gold funds are <strong>passive</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Real estate</strong>: a <strong>local</strong> asset; driven by economy, infrastructure, rates; <strong>no MF real estate schemes</strong> currently; <strong>REITs</strong> are gaining popularity with SEBI support.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Returns</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple</strong> return: 12 → 15 = <strong>25%</strong>; 120 → 135 = <strong>12.5%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Annualised</strong> = simple × 12 ÷ months: 5% in 6 months = <strong>10%</strong>; 3% in 4 months = <strong>9%</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"formula\">CAGR = (Later value ÷ Initial value)<sup>1/n</sup> − 1</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Rs 1,000 to Rs 4,000 in 2 yrs = <strong>100%</strong> CAGR.</li>\n<li>Rs 10,000 at 10% for 3 yrs = <strong>Rs 13,310</strong> (vs <strong>13,000</strong> simple).</li>\n<li>n = <strong>days ÷ 365</strong>.</li>\n<li>With dividends, SEBI uses the <strong>reinvestment method</strong> (reinvest at <strong>ex-dividend NAV</strong>): workbook example <strong>1,000 → 1,080 → 1,152 units</strong>, value <strong>Rs 17,280</strong>, <strong>43.65%</strong> over <strong>1.51 years</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Investor return ≠ scheme return</strong> because of <strong>loads</strong>, <strong>taxes</strong> and <strong>cash-flow timing</strong>; advertised returns <strong>ignore loads</strong>.</li>\n<li>Holding periods: <strong>&lt;1 year = absolute</strong>, <strong>&gt;1 year = CAGR</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rolling returns</strong> = average of many consecutive periods; removes <strong>start/end-date bias</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>No promised returns</strong> except <strong>assured-return schemes</strong>, which need a named <strong>guarantor</strong> in the SID; so far <strong>no assured-return product</strong> has been launched, only <strong>capital protection-oriented</strong> funds.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Risk from the investor's view</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"<strong>Low risk ≠ zero risk</strong>\": liquid and ultra-short funds faced <strong>credit events</strong> in <strong>2018 and 2019</strong>.</li>\n<li>Without segregation, redemptions <strong>raise</strong> the bad paper's share: 5% becomes <strong>10%</strong> after 50% redemptions.</li>\n<li>Arbitrage funds using <strong>paired arbitrage, alpha hedging, merger arbitrage</strong> take views, so they're <strong>not risk-free</strong>.</li>\n<li>Schemes being <strong>wound up</strong> are <strong>listed</strong> (<strong>Clause 37(d)</strong> of the MF Regulations).</li>\n<li>Real estate funds are <strong>high risk</strong> but <strong>less risky than direct property</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Risk measures</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Variance</strong>: fluctuation vs <strong>own average</strong>; workbook example <strong>0.67 vs 39.58</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SD</strong> = <strong>√variance</strong> = <strong>total risk</strong>; for <strong>debt and equity</strong>; needs <strong>≥30 observations</strong>.</li>\n<li>Annualise SD: <strong>×√52</strong> weekly (0.65 → <strong>4.70%</strong>), <strong>×√12</strong> monthly, <strong>×√252</strong> daily.</li>\n<li><strong>Beta</strong> (from <strong>CAPM</strong>) = <strong>systematic</strong> risk vs a diversified index (<strong>β = 1</strong>); <strong>equity only</strong>.</li>\n<li>Beta 0.8 moves <strong>8%</strong> when the market moves 10%; beta 1.2 moves <strong>12%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Modified duration</strong> = interest rate sensitivity; the <strong>professional's</strong> measure.</li>\n<li><strong>WAM</strong> example: 70% × 4 yrs + 30% × 1 yr = <strong>3.1 years</strong>; simpler, used with <strong>lay investors</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Credit rating</strong> profile = default risk; <strong>G-secs and cash have no credit risk</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Credit events, gating and side-pocketing</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Credit event</strong> = <strong>default, delay or downgrade</strong>.</li>\n<li>Rs 10,000 cr scheme with 8% in one paper; 20% redemptions push exposure to <strong>10%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gating</strong> (restricting redemptions): only in <strong>market-wide</strong> crises (liquidity, <strong>exchange closure</strong>, operational <strong>force majeure</strong>); <strong>never for one bad security</strong>.</li>\n<li>Gating: <strong>max 10 working days in any 90 days</strong>; needs <strong>AMC Board + trustee</strong> approval; <strong>SEBI informed immediately</strong>.</li>\n<li>During gating, the <strong>first Rs 2 lakh</strong> of every redemption is paid normally.</li>\n<li><strong>Segregated portfolio</strong> (Dec <strong>2018</strong>): <strong>optional</strong>; triggered by a <strong>downgrade</strong>. Unrated debt can be segregated <strong>only on actual default</strong>, after AMFI informs all AMCs (<strong>7 Nov 2019</strong>).</li>\n<li>New schemes after <strong>7 Nov 2019</strong> must carry <strong>enabling provisions</strong> in the SID.</li>\n<li>Segregation policy approved by <strong>trustees</strong>; effective from the <strong>credit-event day</strong>; <strong>press release</strong> issued.</li>\n<li>Investors get <strong>equal units</strong> in the segregated portfolio; <strong>no subscription or redemption</strong> in it; <strong>listed within 10 working days</strong>.</li>\n<li>New investors get <strong>main-portfolio units only</strong>.</li>\n<li>Segregated portfolio costs: <strong>no management fee</strong>; TER only <strong>on recovery</strong>, capped at the <strong>main portfolio's average</strong>; legal costs within TER, <strong>excess borne by the AMC</strong>; <strong>never charged to the main portfolio</strong>.</li>\n<li>Workbook example: total NAV <strong>1,000.1227</strong> = main <strong>876.2427</strong> + segregated <strong>123.88</strong>.</li>\n<li>Mitigants: <strong>longer holding</strong> (<strong>10 yrs = almost no negative returns</strong> historically); <strong>match holding period to portfolio maturity</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li><strong>SD</strong> = total risk (debt and equity); <strong>beta</strong> = systematic risk only (equity only).</li>\n<li><strong>Systematic</strong> risk is non-diversifiable (e.g. inflation); <strong>unsystematic</strong> is diversifiable (e.g. a strike); investors are rewarded only for <strong>systematic</strong> risk.</li>\n<li>Annualise SD with <strong>√52</strong> weekly, <strong>√12</strong> monthly, <strong>√252</strong> daily, not 52, 12 or 252.</li>\n<li><strong>Technical</strong> analysis suits the short term and timing; <strong>fundamental</strong> analysis suits the long term.</li>\n<li>Dividend yield <strong>falls</strong> in bull markets and <strong>rises</strong> in bear markets.</li>\n<li>Rates expected up: <strong>shorten</strong> duration or buy floaters; rates expected down: buy <strong>long</strong> duration.</li>\n<li>Credit spread = corporate yield − <strong>gilt</strong> yield, not coupon − YTM.</li>\n<li>CP: up to <strong>1 year</strong>; CDs: banks <strong>7 days–1 year</strong>, FIs <strong>1–3 years</strong>.</li>\n<li>Gating only in <strong>market-wide</strong> crises, max <strong>10 working days in any 90 days</strong>; never for one bad security.</li>\n<li>Weaker rupee = <strong>higher</strong> gold fund return; lower LTV = <strong>safer</strong> securitised pool.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-a (True), 2-c (technical), 3-c (12.5%), 4-a (variance).</p>"},{"id":"ch11","n":11,"title":"Mutual Fund Scheme Performance","marks":7,"html":"<h3>Benchmarks</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A benchmark must match the scheme's <strong>investment objective, asset allocation and investment strategy</strong>, and be run by an <strong>independent agency</strong>, transparently.</li>\n<li><strong>Past returns are never</strong> a basis for choosing a benchmark.</li>\n<li>An index fund's benchmark = <strong>the index it tracks</strong>.</li>\n<li>Benchmark is chosen by the <strong>AMC with the trustees</strong>, stated in the <strong>SID</strong>; a change must be <strong>justified and documented</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>TRI since 1 Feb 2018</strong> (was PRI); TRI adds <strong>dividends/interest</strong>.</li>\n<li>TRI makes it look like <strong>fewer schemes beat</strong> the benchmark; the PRI–TRI gap = index dividend yield, <strong>\\~1.5–2.5%</strong>.</li>\n<li>Periods before TRI existed use a <strong>composite CAGR</strong> (PRI then TRI).</li>\n<li>Equity benchmark depends on <strong>scheme type</strong> (diversified vs <strong>sectoral</strong>, e.g. <strong>Bankex</strong>), <strong>universe</strong> (large vs mid cap), <strong>concentration</strong> (fewer stocks = <strong>Sensex 30 / Nifty 50</strong>; more = Nifty 100/200/500).</li>\n<li>Debt indices come from <strong>CRISIL, ICICI Securities, NSE</strong>, recommended by <strong>AMFI</strong>; the list of benchmarks is on <strong>AMFI's website</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>I-Bex</strong> sub-indices: <strong>Si-Bex 1–3 yrs</strong>, <strong>Mi-Bex 3–7 yrs</strong>, <strong>Li-Bex &gt;7 yrs</strong>.</li>\n<li>Debt benchmarks (NSE / CRISIL): overnight <strong>NIFTY 1D Rate / CRISIL Liquid Overnight</strong>; liquid <strong>NIFTY Liquid A-I / CRISIL Liquid Debt A-I</strong>; money market <strong>NIFTY / CRISIL Money Market A-I</strong>; ultra short <strong>Ultra Short Duration Debt A-I</strong>; low duration <strong>Low Duration Debt A-I</strong>; short duration <strong>Short Duration Debt A-II</strong>.</li>\n<li>Liquid schemes: a short-term money market benchmark such as <strong>NSE's Liquid</strong> or <strong>CRISIL Liquid Fund Index</strong>.</li>\n<li>Equity benchmarks (NSE / BSE): multi cap <strong>Nifty 500 Multicap 50:25:25 / BSE 500</strong>; large cap <strong>NIFTY 100 / BSE 100</strong>; large &amp; mid <strong>NIFTY LargeMidcap 250 / BSE 250 LargeMidCap</strong>; mid cap <strong>Midcap 150</strong>; small cap <strong>NIFTY Smallcap 250 / BSE 250 SmallCap</strong>; ELSS, flexi cap, dividend yield <strong>NIFTY 500 / BSE 500</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hybrid benchmark = <strong>blend</strong>, e.g. <strong>65% Sensex + 35% I-Bex</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hybrid benchmarks (NSE, Table 11.2): conservative <strong>NIFTY 50 Hybrid Composite Debt 15:85</strong>; balanced and <strong>BAF/DAA</strong> <strong>50:50</strong>; aggressive <strong>65:35</strong> (or <strong>NIFTY Mid Small cap 400 TRI: 70%</strong>); arbitrage <strong>NIFTY 50 Arbitrage</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gold ETF</strong> benchmark = <strong>price of gold</strong>; international funds use a local index (e.g. <strong>Shanghai Composite</strong>, <strong>S&amp;P 500</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Two-tier benchmarks</strong> (SEBI <strong>Oct 2021</strong>, effective <strong>1 Jan 2022</strong>): Tier 1 = <strong>category</strong>, Tier 2 = <strong>fund manager's style</strong>; applies to <strong>debt and equity</strong> schemes; all must be <strong>TRI</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Single benchmark</strong> for <strong>hybrid/solution, sectoral, index/ETF</strong> schemes.</li>\n<li><strong>FoF</strong>: a single-fund FoF uses the <strong>underlying's</strong> benchmark; a multi-fund FoF uses a <strong>broad market index</strong>.</li>\n<li>Debt Tier-1 benchmarks follow <strong>PRC</strong>, from <strong>1 Apr 2022</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Performance measures</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Relative return</strong> vs benchmark or peers <strong>ignores risk</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>AMCs and trustees</strong> review relative returns <strong>periodically</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"formula\">Sharpe = (R<sub>s</sub> − R<sub>f</sub>) ÷ SD &nbsp;·&nbsp; Treynor = (R<sub>s</sub> − R<sub>f</sub>) ÷ Beta &nbsp;·&nbsp; IR = (R<sub>p</sub> − R<sub>b</sub>) ÷ SD of excess return</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>R<sub>s</sub> − R<sub>f</sub> = <strong>risk premium</strong>; the risk-free rate = <strong>T-bill</strong> return.</li>\n<li><strong>Sharpe</strong> uses <strong>SD</strong> (total risk). Example: (7 − 5) ÷ 0.5 = <strong>4</strong>. Higher = better; compare <strong>like with like</strong> (never equity vs debt).</li>\n<li><strong>Treynor</strong> uses <strong>beta</strong> (systematic risk). Example: (8 − 5) ÷ 1.2 = <strong>2.5</strong>. For <strong>diversified equity</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Alpha</strong> = actual − <strong>beta-expected (optimal)</strong> return; <strong>positive = outperformance</strong>; for diversified equity.</li>\n<li><strong>Information Ratio</strong> = <strong>excess return over the Tier-1 benchmark ÷ SD of excess return</strong> (daily data).</li>\n<li>IR is the <strong>only mandatory</strong> risk-adjusted measure; disclosed <strong>daily</strong> on the AMC and AMFI sites (<strong>Jan 2025</strong> circular).</li>\n<li><strong>Tracking error</strong> = <strong>SD of excess returns</strong>; it shows how closely an <strong>index fund</strong> tracks its index (<strong>ideally zero</strong>) and how <strong>consistently</strong> an active fund beats its benchmark (<strong>lower = more consistent</strong>).</li>\n<li>Risk-adjusted <strong>\"calculations are arithmetic; evaluation is an art\"</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Disclosure sources</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SID</strong> is updated (with performance) <strong>twice a year</strong>; the <strong>factsheet</strong> is <strong>monthly</strong> but <strong>not mandatory</strong>, and ad rules apply to it.</li>\n<li>Product label shows the <strong>objective</strong> (wealth creation / income / liquidity), <strong>asset class</strong>, <strong>holding period</strong> and <strong>risk-o-meter</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Discrete annual returns</strong> show <strong>consistency</strong> across market conditions.</li>\n<li>Factsheet macro readings: high <strong>fiscal deficit</strong> pushes <strong>interest rates up</strong>; <strong>inflation</strong> above comfort brings <strong>monetary tightening</strong>; low <strong>global rates</strong> pull <strong>flows into emerging markets</strong>; <strong>oil</strong> prices move the <strong>currency</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>AMFI website</strong> has performance data for <strong>all schemes</strong>; data vendors include <strong>Morningstar and Value Research</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li><strong>Sharpe</strong> divides by <strong>SD</strong> (total risk); <strong>Treynor</strong> divides by <strong>beta</strong> (systematic risk); both share the numerator (scheme return − T-bill return).</li>\n<li>Information Ratio = excess return over the <strong>Tier-1 benchmark</strong> ÷ SD of that excess return, not (scheme return − T-bill return).</li>\n<li><strong>Tier 1 = category</strong>, <strong>Tier 2 = fund manager's style</strong>, not the other way round.</li>\n<li>TRI since <strong>1 Feb 2018</strong>; two-tier benchmarks effective <strong>1 Jan 2022</strong> (circular Oct 2021); debt Tier-1 on PRC from <strong>1 Apr 2022</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Si-Bex 1–3 yrs</strong>, <strong>Mi-Bex 3–7 yrs</strong>, <strong>Li-Bex &gt;7 yrs</strong>.</li>\n<li>IR is the <strong>only mandatory</strong> risk-adjusted measure, disclosed <strong>daily</strong> on AMC and AMFI sites, not Sharpe and not monthly.</li>\n<li>Factsheet is <strong>monthly but not mandatory</strong>; the SID (with performance) is updated <strong>twice a year</strong>.</li>\n<li>Single-fund FoF uses the <strong>underlying's</strong> benchmark; multi-fund FoF uses a <strong>broad market index</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower</strong> tracking error = more consistent outperformance; an index fund's tracking error is ideally <strong>zero</strong>.</li>\n<li>Benchmark is chosen by the <strong>AMC with the trustees</strong>, never on <strong>past returns</strong>; a change must be justified and documented.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-d (Sharpe), 2-d (past returns), 3-a (TRI), 4-b (tracking error).</p>"},{"id":"ch12","n":12,"title":"Mutual Fund Scheme Selection","marks":15,"html":"<h3>Investor fit</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Step 1: <strong>set goals</strong>; step 2: <strong>assess situation and risk appetite</strong> (<strong>need + ability + willingness</strong>).</li>\n<li>Needs map to funds: <strong>growth → equity</strong>, <strong>income → debt</strong>, <strong>parking → liquid</strong>.</li>\n<li>Equity needs a <strong>long horizon</strong>, even for an investor willing to take risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Age alone is a poor guide</strong>; use the <strong>time horizon to each goal</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Core</strong> = long-term goals (diversified, large, mid cap). <strong>Satellite</strong> = tactical: <strong>sector funds</strong>, <strong>long gilt</strong> (rates expected down), <strong>gold</strong> (high inflation or uncertainty).</li>\n<li>Workbook illustration: a moderate investor at <strong>80% core / 20% satellite</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Risk hierarchies (left to right)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Overall, <strong>risk and return rise</strong>: liquid → debt → hybrid → equity.</li>\n<li><strong>Interest rate risk rises</strong>: overnight → liquid → ultra-short → low → short → medium → medium-long → long.</li>\n<li><strong>Credit risk rises</strong>: <strong>gilt</strong> → banking &amp; PSU → corporate bond → <strong>credit risk fund</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Equity risk falls</strong> in this order: small → mid → multi → large &amp; mid → <strong>large</strong> (lowest).</li>\n<li><strong>Concentration risk rises</strong>: diversified → <strong>focused</strong> → thematic → <strong>sector</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid risk rises</strong>: <strong>arbitrage</strong> → <strong>conservative</strong> → equity savings → dynamic asset allocation → multi-asset → balanced → <strong>aggressive</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Product labelling</strong> (2013) added a disclaimer to <strong>consult a financial adviser</strong> if unsure of suitability; the <strong>risk-o-meter</strong> is covered in Ch 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Choosing by strategy</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Low-risk equity: <strong>index or value</strong> fund. Extra risk for extra return: <strong>growth</strong> fund.</li>\n<li><strong>Passive</strong> = <strong>no selection risk</strong>, lower cost, but <strong>NAV still falls</strong> when the index falls.</li>\n<li><strong>Active</strong> = higher cost and risk, and must <strong>beat the benchmark</strong> to justify the choice.</li>\n<li>Open-ended: liquidity at <strong>NAV less load</strong>, but must hold some <strong>cash</strong> (dilutes returns) and faces <strong>flow pressure</strong>.</li>\n<li>Close-ended: exit only via the exchange, often at a <strong>discount with no limit</strong>; price <strong>converges to NAV near maturity</strong>.</li>\n<li>Close-ended funds can hold <strong>illiquid or long-gestation</strong> stocks; choose them only if the money can stay <strong>locked in</strong>.</li>\n<li>Focused funds are <strong>riskier than diversified</strong> but <strong>less risky than sector</strong> funds.</li>\n<li><strong>Sector</strong> funds depend on <strong>timing</strong>, so they belong in the <strong>satellite</strong> portfolio.</li>\n<li><strong>Thematic</strong> funds (MNC, PSU, infra) sit <strong>between diversified and sector</strong>.</li>\n<li>\"<strong>Diversification is protection against ignorance.</strong>\"</li>\n<li>Mid/small caps shine in <strong>recoveries</strong>, when large caps turn expensive, but many <strong>fail in downturns</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth</strong> outperforms in <strong>bull</strong> markets; <strong>value</strong> outperforms in <strong>falling</strong> markets <strong>or over a long period</strong>, and needs a <strong>long horizon</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>International funds</strong> = foreign equity + <strong>currency</strong>: a <strong>stronger dollar helps</strong> an Indian investor; they suit the <strong>satellite</strong> portfolio.</li>\n<li><strong>FMP</strong>: horizon must <strong>match maturity</strong>, gives more <strong>predictable</strong> returns than FDs, check <strong>credit risk</strong>; exit before maturity <strong>only via exchange</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>TMFs</strong> are replacing FMPs: G-secs, SDLs, <strong>AAA PSU</strong> paper; <strong>liquid</strong> via the AMC.</li>\n<li><strong>Short duration (1–3 yrs)</strong>: use when rates are <strong>expected to rise</strong>; low volatility; core for low-risk investors.</li>\n<li><strong>Liquid fund</strong>: lowest risk, compares to a <strong>savings account</strong>, for parking <strong>up to 91 days</strong>; using it longer <strong>wastes return</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Floater</strong> NAV stays <strong>steady</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Duration-managed</strong> debt funds are <strong>more volatile</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hybrid funds: judge the <strong>equity quality</strong> and the <strong>debt credit/duration</strong> separately; \"<strong>conservative\" refers only to equity allocation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Be <strong>cautious with dynamic asset allocation</strong> funds.</li>\n<li>A balanced fund's tax status depends on its <strong>equity share</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gold ETF</strong> tracks the <strong>gold price</strong>. A <strong>gold sector fund</strong> holds <strong>mining shares</strong>, which rise and fall <strong>more</strong> and move with company factors.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Choosing within a category</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Check the portfolio is <strong>true to label</strong> (e.g. a \"value\" fund full of expensive front-line stocks isn't).</li>\n<li><strong>Performance</strong>: <strong>consistent</strong> outperformance, <strong>downside protection</strong>, vs <strong>benchmark and peers</strong>, over <strong>rolling</strong> periods.</li>\n<li>Look-back: <strong>equity ≥ 5 yrs</strong>; <strong>long-term debt ≥ 3 yrs</strong>; <strong>short-term debt</strong> shorter; <strong>liquid funds</strong> use <strong>7 days, 15 days, 1 month</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fund age</strong> matters most where scheme returns <strong>diverge</strong> (e.g. <strong>multi cap</strong> more than large cap).</li>\n<li><strong>Fund size</strong>: large helps <strong>large-cap</strong> funds; hurts <strong>mid-cap or sector</strong> funds with limited stocks.</li>\n<li><strong>Portfolio turnover</strong> = purchases <strong>or</strong> sales (<strong>whichever is lower</strong>) ÷ average net assets: <strong>10,000 ÷ 5,000 = 200%</strong>, average holding <strong>6 months</strong>.</li>\n<li>Judge turnover against <strong>style</strong>: 6 months is too short for <strong>value</strong>, fine for <strong>momentum</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Costs</strong> matter most in <strong>debt</strong> and <strong>index</strong> funds.</li>\n<li>Star ratings = <strong>past performance</strong>, <strong>no predictive</strong> power; this quarter's best fund is rarely next quarter's.</li>\n<li>Target the <strong>top few</strong> consistently: <strong>top 3–5</strong> in small categories, <strong>top 10–15%</strong> in big ones.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Options and do's and don'ts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IDCW payout</strong> gives cash flow but is <strong>not assured</strong> (needs distributable surplus); taxed at <strong>slab</strong>.</li>\n<li>For regular income, <strong>SWP is better</strong> than IDCW (capital gains tax, plus <strong>STT</strong> on equity).</li>\n<li>IDCW suits only <strong>tax-exempt</strong> investors; <strong>growth</strong> compounds <strong>pre-tax</strong>.</li>\n<li>Book gains or losses to <strong>set off</strong> against others.</li>\n<li>Do: ensure <strong>suitability</strong> (<strong>PFUTP</strong>), stick to <strong>asset allocation</strong>, understand <strong>objective and strategy</strong>, watch <strong>taxes and loads</strong> on redemption, keep a <strong>written, consistent</strong> selection method.</li>\n<li>Don't: <strong>chase past performance</strong>.</li>\n<li>Code of conduct for AMCs and trustees = <strong>Fourth Schedule</strong> (Appendix 1): <strong>equitable treatment</strong> of all unitholder classes (favouring one set is not fair), no excessive concentration with brokers or <strong>few investors</strong>.</li>\n<li>Regulated entities and their agents (incl. <strong>MFDs</strong>) must show their <strong>registered name and registration number</strong> on their <strong>social media home page</strong> and at the <strong>start of every video/content</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Exam traps</h3>\n<ul class=\"traps\">\n<li>Equity risk is <strong>lowest in large cap</strong>, then large &amp; mid, multi, mid, and <strong>highest in small cap</strong>; large &amp; mid is less risky than multi cap, not more.</li>\n<li>Credit risk rises <strong>gilt → banking &amp; PSU → corporate bond → credit risk fund</strong>; banking &amp; PSU is below corporate bond, not above.</li>\n<li>Concentration risk: diversified &lt; focused &lt; <strong>thematic</strong> &lt; <strong>sector</strong>; thematic sits between diversified and sector, not above sector.</li>\n<li>Hybrid risk starts at <strong>arbitrage</strong> (lowest), not conservative, and ends at <strong>aggressive</strong>, which is above balanced.</li>\n<li>Long gilt is a satellite bet when rates are expected to <strong>fall</strong>; short duration (1-3 yrs) is used when rates are expected to <strong>rise</strong>.</li>\n<li>Liquid funds are for parking <strong>up to 91 days</strong>, not longer; holding them longer wastes return.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth</strong> outperforms in <strong>bull</strong> markets; <strong>value</strong> outperforms in <strong>falling</strong> markets or over a long period, not in bull runs.</li>\n<li>A <strong>stronger</strong> dollar helps an Indian investor in an international fund, not a weaker one; international funds are <strong>satellite</strong>, not core.</li>\n<li>Turnover numerator = <strong>lower</strong> of purchases or sales, not the higher; 200% turnover = about <strong>6 months</strong> holding (too short for value, fine for momentum).</li>\n<li>Look-back: equity <strong>at least 5 yrs</strong>, long-term debt <strong>at least 3 yrs</strong>; target top <strong>3-5</strong> in small categories, top <strong>10-15%</strong> in big ones, not the reverse.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workbook sample answers:</strong> 1-d (gilt), 2-a (risk-o-meter), 3-b (False), 4-a (multi-cap), 5-a (FMPs), 6-a (True), 7-c (both).</p>"}];
